var e=JSON.parse('[[2101,"`useEffect` combines the logic of `componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate` and `componentWillUnmount`. An empty array of dependencies (`[]`) means the effect will run only on mounting and will clean up on unmounting.","The analogies with classes:\\n- `componentDidMount`: an effect with `[]`.\\n- `componentDidUpdate`: an effect without `[]` or with dependencies.\\n- `componentWillUnmount`: the clean-up function in `useEffect`.\\n\\nExamples:\\n- Mounting and unmounting:\\n\\n- Dependencies:\\n\\nAn empty array of dependencies:\\n- The effect runs once, after the first render.\\n- The clean-up (if there is any) is performed on unmounting.\\n\\nConclusion: `useEffect` unifies the work with side effects, replacing several lifecycle methods.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\'The component has mounted\');\\n  return () => console.log(\'The component has unmounted\');\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\'The value has changed:\', value);\\n}, [value]); // It fires when `value` changes"],[2102,"- `useMemo`: it caches the result of a computation, recomputing it only when the dependencies change.\\n- `useCallback`: it caches a function, preventing it from being recreated.\\nBoth help you avoid unnecessary re-renders.","1. `useMemo`:\\n- It is used for heavy computations.\\n- An example:\\n\\n2. `useCallback`:\\n- It keeps the reference to a function between renders.\\n- An example:\\n\\nHow memoization works:\\n- React remembers the value/function and returns it as long as the dependencies have not changed.\\n- Without memoization, new objects/functions are created on every render, which can cause unnecessary re-renders of the child components.\\n\\nConclusion: use `useMemo` for values and `useCallback` for functions, if they are passed down to optimized child components (`React.memo`, for example).","const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b]);\\n\\nconst handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(a, b), [a, b]);"],[2103,"Fragments (`<>...</>` or `<React.Fragment>`) let you group elements without adding an unnecessary DOM node. This is useful for returning several elements from a component.","The problem without fragments: a component can return only one root element. You used to have to use a `<div>`, which complicated the DOM structure.\\n\\nThe solution\\n\\nThe key characteristics:\\n- It creates no unnecessary elements in the DOM.\\n- It can have a key (`<React.Fragment key={...}>`).\\n\\nConclusion: fragments make markup easier, especially in lists and tables.","function List() {\\n  return (\\n    <> {/* A fragment */}\\n      <li>Item 1</li>\\n      <li>Item 2</li>\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2104,"- Use `React.memo` in order to memoize the component.\\n- Pass primitive props (numbers, strings).\\n- For complex objects — `useMemo`/`useCallback`.","1. `React.memo`:\\n- The component will re-render only if its props have changed.\\n\\n2. Primitive props:\\n- The comparison is fast (by value).\\n\\n3. Memoizing functions/objects:\\n\\nConclusion: combine `React.memo`, primitives and `useMemo`/`useCallback`.","const MemoizedComponent = React.memo(MyComponent);\\n\\n<Component count={5} text=\\"Hello\\" />  // Optimal\\n\\nconst data = useMemo(() => ({ id: 1 }), []);\\nconst onClick = useCallback(() => {}, []);"],[2105,"Primitives (strings, numbers) are compared by value, while objects/arrays are compared by reference. That makes checking for changes faster.","How the comparison works:\\n- Primitives:\\n\\n- Objects:\\n\\nAn example with `React.memo`:\\n\\nConclusion: primitives are the most efficient kind of prop for memoization.","5 === 5 // true\\n\'text\' === \'text\' // true\\n\\n{} === {} // false (different references)\\n\\n// A re-render only if `count` or `text` has changed:\\n<MemoizedComponent count={5} text=\\"Hello\\" />\\n\\n// A re-render on every render of the parent (a new object):\\n<MemoizedComponent config={{ id: 1 }} />"],[2106,"`React.memo` compares the props pairwise (prevProps ↔ nextProps) through `Object.is`. If every prop is equal, there is no re-render.","The `React.memo` algorithm:\\n- It receives the previous (`prevProps`) and the new (`nextProps`) props.\\n- It compares each key through `Object.is`:\\n- For primitives: by value.\\n- For objects: by reference.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nCustomizing the comparison: you can pass a function of your own:\\n\\nConclusion: by default `React.memo` performs a shallow comparison, which is fast but goes no deeper than one level.","const props1 = { count: 5, data: { id: 1 } };\\nconst props2 = { count: 5, data: { id: 1 } };\\n\\n// The shallow comparison:\\nprops1.count === props2.count // true\\nprops1.data === props2.data // false (different references)\\n\\nReact.memo(Component, (prevProps, nextProps) => {\\n  return prevProps.id === nextProps.id; // A re-render only if the id has changed\\n});"],[2107,"FSD divides a project into layers by business logic:\\n- App — initialization, routing.\\n- Pages — the pages.\\n- Features — the features (authorization, for example).\\n- Entities — the business entities (User, Product).\\n- Shared — the UI kit, the utilities.","An example of the structure:\\n\\nThe advantages of FSD:\\n- A clear separation of the areas of responsibility.\\n- Scalability for large projects.\\n- The reuse of code.\\n\\nConclusion: FSD is an alternative to Atomic Design, oriented towards business logic.","src/\\n├── app/               # The application\'s initialization\\n├── pages/             # The pages (HomePage, for example)\\n├── features/          # The features (AuthByPhone, for example)\\n├── entities/          # The entities (User, for example)\\n└── shared/            # The shared components (Button, API)"],[2108,"An accordion with infinite nesting is implemented by means of a recursive component that renders itself for each level of nesting. For optimization, memoization (`React.memo`) is used in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders, together with the lazy loading of the content.","Implementing the accordion: a recursive component takes an array of data and, for each item, renders a heading and the content. If an item has nested items, the component calls itself.\\n\\nOptimizing the rendering:\\n- `React.memo`: wrap the component in `React.memo` in order to prevent a re-render when the props are unchanged.\\n- `useCallback`: for handler functions, use `useCallback` in order to keep the references between renders.\\n- Virtualization: for very large trees, use virtualization libraries (`react-virtualized`, for example) in order to render only the visible items.\\n- Lazy loading: load the nested content only when the accordion is opened.\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\n- For tree-shaped data structures (menus, categories, file systems).\\n- When the nesting can be dynamic and is not known at development time.","const AccordionItem = ({ item }) => {\\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}>\\n        {item.title}\\n      </button>\\n      {isOpen && (\\n        <div>\\n          <p>{item.content}</p>\\n          {item.children?.map(child => (\\n            <AccordionItem key={child.id} item={child} />\\n          ))}\\n        </div>\\n      )}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2109,"Using an array index as a key can lead to bugs in the display of components\' state when the order of the items changes (when they are added, removed or sorted). React may match the elements and their state up incorrectly.","The problems with an index as a key:\\n- When items are added or removed the indexes change, and React may confuse the elements.\\n- A component\'s state (the text typed into a field, for example) can \\"move\\" to a different element.\\n- Performance can drop, since React will re-render more elements than it needs to.\\n\\nAn example: suppose there is a to-do list. If you delete the first to-do, the second one\'s index becomes 0, and React may keep the state (the text, for example) for the first to-do but apply it to the second.\\n\\nWhen an index can be used:\\n- Only if the list is static (the items do not change order).\\n- If the items have no identifiers and no state.\\n\\nThe best practice: always use unique IDs from your data. If there are none, create them from the data (a hash of the content, for example).",null],[2110,"For large lists use virtualization — the technique in which only the visible items are rendered. It improves performance and memory consumption considerably.","The problem: rendering thousands of items at once leads to a sluggish interface and a heavy load on the browser.\\n\\nThe solution — virtualization: virtualization displays only the items that lie in the viewport (the visible area). The other items are not rendered until the user scrolls.\\n\\nHow to implement it: use libraries:\\n- `react-window`\\n- `react-virtualized`\\n\\nAn example with `react-window`:\\n\\nThe alternative methods:\\n- Pagination: dividing the list into pages.\\n- Infinite scroll: loading the data as the user scrolls.\\n- Optimizing the components: memoizing the list\'s items.\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\n- When working with lists of 100+ items.\\n- When performance on mobile devices matters.","import { FixedSizeList as List } from \'react-window\';\\n\\nconst Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n  <div style={style}>\\n    {items[index].name}\\n  </div>\\n);\\n\\nconst VirtualizedList = () => (\\n  <List\\n    height={400}\\n    itemCount={items.length}\\n    itemSize={50}\\n  >\\n    {Row}\\n  </List>\\n);"],[2111,"A modal window is implemented as a component that is displayed over the main content. Handling a click outside the modal is done by checking the event\'s target: if the click was not on the modal, we close it.","The structure of a modal window:\\n- The modal component is rendered in a separate portal (in `document.body`, for example) in order to avoid problems with z-index.\\n- Its visibility state is managed through React state.\\n\\nHandling an outside click:\\n- We add a click handler to the document when the modal opens.\\n- In the handler we check whether the click was inside the modal (using a `ref`).\\n- If the click was outside, we close the modal.\\n\\nAn example implementation:\\n\\nImprovements:\\n- Adding appearance/disappearance animations.\\n- Handling the Escape key for closing.\\n- Blocking the scrolling of the page\'s body while the modal is open.","const Modal = ({ isOpen, onClose, children }) => {\\n  const modalRef = useRef();\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleClickOutside = (event) => {\\n      if (modalRef.current && !modalRef.current.contains(event.target)) {\\n        onClose();\\n      }\\n    };\\n\\n    if (isOpen) {\\n      document.addEventListener(\'mousedown\', handleClickOutside);\\n    }\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      document.removeEventListener(\'mousedown\', handleClickOutside);\\n    };\\n  }, [isOpen, onClose]);\\n\\n  if (!isOpen) return null;\\n\\n  return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n    <div className=\\"modal-overlay\\">\\n      <div ref={modalRef} className=\\"modal-content\\">\\n        {children}\\n        <form>\\n          <input type=\\"text\\" placeholder=\\"Login\\" />\\n          <input type=\\"password\\" placeholder=\\"Password\\" />\\n          <button type=\\"submit\\">Login</button>\\n        </form>\\n      </div>\\n    </div>,\\n    document.body\\n  );\\n};"],[2112,"To prevent the page from reloading when a form is submitted, you have to cancel the submit event\'s default behaviour. In React this is done by calling `event.preventDefault()` in the form\'s submit handler.","The default behaviour: by default, when a form is submitted the browser tries to send the data to the server and reloads the page. In modern SPA applications that behaviour is undesirable.\\n\\nHow to cancel it: in the form\'s `onSubmit` event handler you have to call `event.preventDefault()`.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhat next: once the reload has been cancelled you can:\\n- Validate the form\'s data.\\n- Send the data to the server by means of AJAX (through `fetch` or `axios`, for example).\\n- Update the application\'s state from the server\'s response.\\n\\nImportant: do not forget to handle errors when sending the data and to show the user some feedback.","const LoginForm = () => {\\n  const handleSubmit = (event) => {\\n    event.preventDefault(); // We prevent the reload\\n    // Your logic for handling the form\'s data goes here\\n    console.log(\'The form was submitted, but the page did not reload\');\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n      <input type=\\"text\\" name=\\"username\\" />\\n      <input type=\\"password\\" name=\\"password\\" />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Login</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n};"],[2113,"To stop the event propagating, you have to call the `event.stopPropagation()` method in the event handler. That will prevent the event from being passed to the parent elements","Event bubbling: by default events in the DOM bubble up from the target element through the tree to the root of the document. That can cause handlers on the parent elements to fire when you do not want them to.\\n\\nHow to stop it: in the event handler call `event.stopPropagation()`.\\n\\nAn example with a modal window: suppose you have a button inside the modal, and you do not want a click on it to close the modal (which is implemented through an outside click).\\n\\nImportant points:\\n- `stopPropagation()` stops the bubbling only for that particular event.\\n- The alternative is to check `event.target` in the parent\'s handler, but that is less reliable.\\n- Do not overuse `stopPropagation()`, since it can break the expected behaviour of other components.","const Modal = ({ onClose }) => {\\n  const handleInnerClick = (event) => {\\n    event.stopPropagation(); // We stop the bubbling\\n    // Your logic for handling a click inside the modal\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"modal-overlay\\" onClick={onClose}>\\n      <div className=\\"modal-content\\" onClick={handleInnerClick}>\\n        {/* The modal\'s content */}\\n        <button>A button inside the modal</button>\\n      </div>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2114,"To unify the handler, use the `name` attribute on the form fields and a single `onChange` handler, which updates the corresponding value in the state by the field\'s name.","The problem: in forms with many fields, creating a separate handler for each field leads to duplicated code and complicates the component.\\n\\nThe solution: create a single handler that will determine which field has changed and update the state.\\n\\nAn example implementation:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n- One handler for every field.\\n- It is easy to add new fields without changing the logic.\\n- The code becomes cleaner and easier to maintain\\n\\nAn extension: for complex forms with nested data you can use libraries such as Formik or React Hook Form, which provide ready-made solutions for managing a form\'s state.","const UnifiedForm = () => {\\n  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({\\n    username: \'\',\\n    email: \'\',\\n    password: \'\'\\n  });\\n\\n  const handleChange = (event) => {\\n    const { name, value } = event.target;\\n    setFormData(prevState => ({\\n      ...prevState,\\n      [name]: value\\n    }));\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      <input\\n        type=\\"text\\"\\n        name=\\"username\\"\\n        value={formData.username}\\n        onChange={handleChange}\\n      />\\n      <input\\n        type=\\"email\\"\\n        name=\\"email\\"\\n        value={formData.email}\\n        onChange={handleChange}\\n      />\\n      <input\\n        type=\\"password\\"\\n        name=\\"password\\"\\n        value={formData.password}\\n        onChange={handleChange}\\n      />\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n};"],[2115,"Yes, any mutable value can be held in a ref. A ref is a container for data that can change but must not trigger a re-render when it does.","What a ref is: a ref is an object with a `current` property, which can contain any value. Unlike state, changing a ref does not cause the component to render again.\\n\\nWhat can be held in a ref:\\n- References to DOM elements\\n- Timers and intervals\\n- Any mutable values\\n- The previous values of state\\n- Complex computations that must not trigger a render\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nImportant characteristics:\\n- Changing a ref does not trigger a re-render\\n- A ref is preserved between a component\'s renders\\n- A ref\'s value is available immediately after an update\\n\\nWhen to use a ref instead of state:\\n- For values that do not affect the display\\n- For holding previous values\\n- For timers and event subscriptions","function TimerComponent() {\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n  \\n  const startTimer = () => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      console.log(\'Timer tick\');\\n    }, 1000);\\n  };\\n  \\n  const stopTimer = () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n  };\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={startTimer}>Start</button>\\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2116,"Redux Toolkit provides predictable state management with immutable updates. MobX uses observable objects for reactive programming. React Query specializes in managing server state and caching.","Redux Toolkit:\\n- A centralized state store\\n- Immutable updates through reducers\\n- Built-in support for asynchronous operations\\n- Developer tools for debugging\\n\\nMobX:\\n- Reactive programming\\n- Automatic dependency tracking\\n- Minimal boilerplate code\\n- A gentle learning curve\\n\\nReact Query:\\n- A specialization in server state\\n- Automatic caching and invalidation\\n- Background updates of data\\n- Pagination and infinite queries\\n\\nA Redux Toolkit example:\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n- Redux Toolkit for complex client state\\n- MobX for reactive applications\\n- React Query for working with APIs and caching","import { createSlice } from \'@reduxjs/toolkit\';\\n\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: \'counter\',\\n  initialState: 0,\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment: state => state + 1,\\n    decrement: state => state - 1\\n  }\\n});"],[2117,"React Hook Form provides minimal boilerplate and high performance through uncontrolled components. Formik offers a more declarative approach with controlled components and built-in validation","React Hook Form:\\n- Uncontrolled components\\n- Minimal re-rendering\\n- Easy integration with validation\\n- Native TypeScript support\\n\\nFormik:\\n- Controlled components\\n- Built-in validation through Yup\\n- A more declarative approach\\n- A rich ecosystem\\n\\nA React Hook Form example:\\n\\nA comparison of performance:\\n- React Hook Form: fewer re-renders\\n- Formik: more control over the state\\n\\nChoosing a library:\\n- For performance: React Hook Form\\n- For complex forms with validation: Formik\\n- For simple forms: a native solution","import { useForm } from \'react-hook-form\';\\n\\nfunction Form() {\\n  const { register, handleSubmit } = useForm();\\n  \\n  const onSubmit = data => console.log(data);\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>\\n      <input {...register(\'firstName\')} />\\n      <input {...register(\'lastName\')} />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2118,"Dynamic forms are implemented by rendering the fields from the state. To manage complex logic, use libraries such as React Hook Form or Formik with dynamic field arrays.","The key capabilities:\\n- Adding/removing fields dynamically\\n- Conditional validation\\n- Dependent fields\\n- Group validation\\n\\nAn example with React Hook Form:\\n\\nThe characteristics of the implementation:\\n- Unique keys for the dynamic fields\\n- Validation on the fly\\n- Performance optimization\\n- Preserving the state through dynamic changes\\n\\nThe best practices:\\n- Use Field Arrays for repeating groups\\n- Implement conditional validation\\n- Optimize the re-renders\\n- Test the edge cases","import { useFieldArray } from \'react-hook-form\';\\n\\nfunction DynamicForm() {\\n  const { control, register } = useForm();\\n  const { fields, append, remove } = useFieldArray({\\n    control,\\n    name: \'users\'\\n  });\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      {fields.map((field, index) => (\\n        <div key={field.id}>\\n          <input {...register(`users.${index}.firstName`)} />\\n          <button onClick={() => remove(index)}>Remove</button>\\n        </div>\\n      ))}\\n      <button onClick={() => append({ firstName: \'\' })}>Add</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2119,"To integrate maps, use the JavaScript APIs of the mapping services (Google Maps, Yandex Maps, Leaflet). Initialize the map in a component and add the markers and the event handlers.","The popular solutions:\\n- The Google Maps JavaScript API\\n- The Yandex Maps API\\n- Leaflet (open source)\\n- Mapbox GL JS\\n\\nThe basic integration:\\n\\nReact integration: use wrapper libraries:\\n- `@react-google-maps/api`\\n- `react-yandex-maps`\\n- `react-leaflet`\\n\\nThe key features:\\n- Adding markers\\n- Drawing shapes and zones\\n- Geocoding addresses\\n- Routing and directions\\n- Custom map styles\\n\\nOptimization:\\n- Lazy loading of the maps\\n- Clustering the markers\\n- Performance optimization\\n- Caching the requests","<div id=\\"map\\" style=\\"width: 100%; height: 400px;\\"></div>\\n\\n<script>\\n  const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById(\'map\'), {\\n    center: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\\n    zoom: 10\\n  });\\n  \\n  new google.maps.Marker({\\n    position: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\\n    map: map,\\n    title: \'Hello World!\'\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[2120,"For drawing on the web, use the Canvas API for pixel graphics or SVG for vector graphics. The popular libraries are: D3.js for complex visualizations, Fabric.js for interactive drawings, Three.js for 3D graphics.","The drawing technologies:\\n- Canvas: pixel graphics, high performance\\n- SVG: vector graphics, scaling without loss\\n- WebGL: 3D graphics through Canvas\\n\\nThe popular libraries:\\n- D3.js: complex data visualizations\\n- Fabric.js: interactive drawings and editors\\n- Three.js: 3D graphics and animation\\n- Konva.js: 2D graphics for React\\n\\nAn example of using Canvas:\\n\\nReact integration:\\n\\nUses:\\n- Dashboards and analytics\\n- Graphics editors\\n- Interactive infographics\\n- Games and simulations\\n\\nBest practices:\\n- Performance optimization\\n- Responsiveness across different devices\\n- Accessibility for users\\n- Caching complex computations","const canvas = document.getElementById(\'myCanvas\');\\nconst ctx = canvas.getContext(\'2d\');\\n\\nctx.fillStyle = \'red\';\\nctx.fillRect(10, 10, 100, 100);\\n\\nimport { Stage, Layer, Circle } from \'react-konva\';\\n\\nfunction DrawingApp() {\\n  return (\\n    <Stage width={window.innerWidth} height={window.innerHeight}>\\n      <Layer>\\n        <Circle x={100} y={100} radius={50} fill=\\"red\\" />\\n      </Layer>\\n    </Stage>\\n  );\\n}"],[2121,"Next.js provides built-in solutions for server-side rendering (SSR), static site generation (SSG), routing, image optimization and API routes, which speeds development up and improves SEO and performance compared with setting those systems up by hand in a React application.","Next.js is a framework built on top of React, which solves the common tasks of web development out of the box.\\n\\nThe key advantages:\\n- Built-in SSR and SSG:\\n- It saves you from having to set up complicated Webpack configurations or Node.js servers for server-side rendering.\\n- It makes it easier to build hybrid applications, where some pages are rendered on the server and others on the client.\\n\\n- File-based routing:\\n- The routing is based on the structure of the folders in the project (`pages/` or `app/`), which is intuitive and reduces the amount of boilerplate code.\\n- It supports dynamic routes and nested layouts.\\n\\n- Out-of-the-box optimizations:\\n- Code splitting: it breaks the code into chunks automatically, for fast loading.\\n- Image optimization: the `next/image` component compresses images, converts them into modern formats and lazy-loads them automatically.\\n- Fonts: optimizing and loading web fonts.\\n- Styles: support for CSS-in-JS, CSS modules, Sass and Tailwind CSS.\\n\\n- API Routes:\\n- It lets you write backend logic (API endpoints) in the same project as the frontend, without standing up a separate server.\\n\\n- A better Developer Experience (DX):\\n- Hot Code Reloading.\\n- Fast builds and advanced error handling.\\n\\nWhen to choose Next.js over React:\\n- For sites where SEO matters (blogs, online shops, media).\\n- When you need high performance through SSR/SSG.\\n- To speed development up thanks to the built-in solutions.",null],[2122,"Promises are closely related to the Event Loop. When a promise is created, its asynchronous operation (a request to the server, for example) is handed to a browser API and performed outside the main thread. The Event Loop constantly checks whether that operation has finished. When it does, the promise\'s callback functions (`.then()`, `.catch()`) are put in the Microtask Queue, which has the highest priority. The Event Loop will run every callback from that queue immediately after the current synchronous code has run, before moving on to anything else.","The relationship between promises and the Event Loop is a fundamental concept for understanding asynchrony in JavaScript.\\n\\n1. The Event Loop\'s role\\nThe Event Loop is the mechanism that continuously checks whether there are tasks to be performed. Its job is to take tasks from the queues and put them on the Call Stack when that is empty.\\n\\n2. A promise\'s lifecycle\\n- Creation and execution: when you create a new promise and start an asynchronous operation (`fetch`, for example), that operation is handed from JavaScript\'s main thread to the browser\'s environment (the Web API), where it is performed.\\n- Waiting: while the operation is being performed, JavaScript\'s main thread is free and can do other things.\\n- Completion: as soon as the operation finishes (successfully or with an error), that promise\'s callback functions (the ones given in `.then()` or `.catch()`) are not run immediately. Instead they are put in a special queue, the Microtask Queue.\\n\\n3. The priority of the microtask queue\\n- Once the Call Stack has been cleared completely (all of the current synchronous code has run), the Event Loop turns to the queues.\\n- An important rule: the Event Loop first runs every task in the microtask queue, and only then moves on to the rendering queue or the macrotask queue (`setTimeout`, for example).\\n- That means promise callbacks have a high priority and are run almost immediately after the asynchronous operation finishes, without waiting for other events.\\n\\nAn example:","console.log(\'Start\'); // 1. Synchronous code\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\'Timeout\'), 0); // 2. A macrotask -> into the macrotask queue\\n\\nPromise.resolve()\\n  .then(() => console.log(\'Promise\')); // 3. A microtask -> into the microtask queue\\n\\nconsole.log(\'End\'); // 4. Synchronous code\\n\\n// Output:\\n// \\"Start\\"\\n// \\"End\\"\\n// \\"Promise\\"  -> it ran first, since microtasks take priority\\n// \\"Timeout\\""],[2123,"React Context is a mechanism built into React for passing data through the component tree without having to write props at every level. Redux is an independent library for managing a whole application\'s state, which provides strict rules for changing it (through actions and reducers) and powerful debugging tools. Context solves the problem of \\"prop drilling\\", while Redux solves the problem of managing complex global state predictably.","Both tools help you work with data, but they do so at different levels and with different aims.\\n\\nReact Context\\n- What it is: a mechanism for passing data straight through. It lets you create a \\"context\\" (a source of data) at the top level and consume that data in any child component, wherever it may sit, bypassing the intermediate components.\\n- How it is used: it is created by means of `React.createContext()`. A provider (`<MyContext.Provider>`) wraps part of the tree and passes a value down. The consumer components use the `useContext(MyContext)` hook in order to access that value.\\n- The main aim: avoiding \\"prop drilling\\" (passing props through a great many unrelated components).\\n\\nRedux\\n- What it is: a library for managing an application\'s state, based on the principles of Flux. It holds all of the state in a single immutable store.\\n- How it is used: the state is changed only through \\"actions\\" and \\"reducers\\" — pure functions that compute the new state predictably from the previous one and the action.\\n- The main aim: to provide a predictable, centralized and debuggable way of managing complex state that is actively used in different parts of an application.",null],[2124,"Context is not intended for updating large volumes of data frequently. When a value in a context changes, React re-renders every component that consumes it, even if they use only the part of the data that has not changed. Redux solves this problem by means of \\"subscriptions\\": a component re-renders only when precisely the data it is subscribed to changes. That gives a performance optimization in large applications","Although Context copes excellently with passing static or rarely changing data down (the UI theme or the user\'s data, for example), using it for frequently changing state has serious disadvantages.\\n\\nThe problems with using Context for global state:\\n- There are no selectors: a component using `useContext` re-renders on any change to the context\'s value, even if it needs only a small part of that data. There is no mechanism for subscribing to a particular field alone.\\n- Optimization is difficult: to avoid unnecessary re-renders you end up having to split the contexts into smaller ones by hand (`UserContext`, `PostsContext`, `UiContext`) or to use complicated wrappers with `React.memo`. That increases the complexity of the code.\\n- The state-update logic: in Context the state-update logic (the equivalent of Redux\'s reducers) has to be implemented by hand, usually by means of `useState` or `useReducer` inside the provider.\\n\\nThe optimizations Redux gives:\\n- Subscribing to particular data: the `react-redux` library provides the `useSelector` hook, which lets a component subscribe to just one particular piece of the state in the store. The component will be re-rendered only if that particular piece has changed.\\n- Stability of references: by default `useSelector` uses strict comparison (`===`), which prevents unnecessary re-renders if the data has not changed.\\n- A Single Source of Truth: Redux\'s architecture is predictable and makes debugging easier. It is easy to trace which action led to a change of state.\\n- Middleware: Redux provides a powerful middleware mechanism (`redux-thunk`, `redux-saga`, for example) for handling side effects (asynchronous requests, logging and so on) in a uniform way.\\n\\nConclusion: Context is an excellent tool for its own niche (data passed straight through that rarely changes). Redux is a specialized, optimized library for managing the complex, frequently changing state of a large application, where performance and predictability are critical.",null],[2125,"React 19 brought a new hook, `use`, but the main hooks for memoization (`useMemo`, `useCallback`, `memo`) remain and work on the same principles. The key change has to do with the React compiler, which has been announced as part of the forthcoming React 19. That compiler will be able to memoize components\' code automatically, which may reduce the need to use `useMemo` and `useCallback` by hand in the future.","The concept of memoization itself has not changed — it is still an optimization for caching computations and preventing unnecessary recomputation and re-renders.\\n\\nWhat has stayed the same:\\n- `useMemo`: for memoizing the results of heavy computations.\\n- `useCallback`: for memoizing functions and passing stable references down to child components.\\n- `React.memo`: for memoizing components and preventing them from re-rendering when their props are unchanged.\\n\\nWhat is new in React 19 and in the future:\\n- The `use` hook:\\nThis is a new hook for reading resources (promises or context, for example). While it does not replace `useMemo`/`useCallback` directly, it represents a new primitive for working with asynchrony, which can affect the overall architecture and the need for memoization.\\n- The React Compiler (the main change):\\nThe most significant \\"change\\" has to do not with the API but with a tool called the React Compiler. Formerly known as \\"React Forget\\", it is a compiler that analyses components\' code automatically and decides where memoization needs to be applied.\\n- The aim: to save developers from having to place `useMemo` and `useCallback` everywhere by hand, reducing the amount of boilerplate code and preventing human error.\\n- How it works: the compiler analyses the code statically and, at build time, adds memoization wherever it is necessary in order to preserve the identity of references and prevent unnecessary computation.\\n\\nConclusion: while the React Compiler has not become the standard, the rules of manual memoization by means of `useMemo`, `useCallback` and `memo` remain relevant and important to performance. React 19 lays the foundation for that work being automated in the future.",null],[2126,"The `useId` hook generates a unique, stable identifier that stays the same both on the client and on the server. You cannot generate a random ID (through `Math.random()`, for example), because that would lead to a mismatch between the HTML code generated on the server (SSR) and what React then paints on the client. It would cause hydration errors and could break the link between form elements and their labels","The problem of unique IDs is especially acute in React applications that use server-side rendering (SSR).\\n\\nWhat `useId` is for:\\n- Its main purpose is to generate unique IDs for HTML elements that have to be linked for accessibility (a11y) purposes — to link a `<label>` tag with an `<input>` element through the `htmlFor` / `for` attribute, for example.\\n- `useId` guarantees that the ID generated will be the same during server-side rendering and on the client during hydration.\\n\\nWhy random generation is bad:\\n- A mismatch in SSR: when rendering on the server an HTML document is generated. If the ID is created through `Math.random()`, the server will generate one ID (\\"input-0.12345\\", for example).\\n- A hydration error: when React on the client tries to \\"hydrate\\" that page (to add the event handlers and make it interactive), it will run the same code. `Math.random()` will generate a completely different value (\\"input-0.67890\\", for example).\\n- The consequences: React will see that the element\'s `id` attribute does not match what was expected and will throw a hydration error. The link between the `<label>` and the `<input>` will be broken, which will make accessibility and the user experience worse.\\n\\nAn example of the correct usage:\\n\\nConclusion: always use `useId` to generate IDs in React components. It is the only way of guaranteeing stability and consistency between server and client rendering, which avoids errors and ensures proper accessibility.","import { useId } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction EmailForm() {\\n  const id = useId(); // It generates a unique, stable ID\\n\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <label htmlFor={id}>Email</label>\\n      <input id={id} type=\\"email\\" />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n// The same ID will be generated on the server and on the client, \\":r1:\\" for example"],[2127,"Atomic Design is a design methodology that divides an interface into hierarchical levels: atoms (a button, an input), molecules (a search bar = an input + a button), organisms (a site\'s header), templates and pages. FSD (Feature-Sliced Design) is an architectural methodology for frontend projects, which focuses on organizing the folders by business logic: it divides the code into layers (app, pages, features, entities, shared). The key difference: Atomic Design is about components, whereas FSD is about a project\'s whole structure, including the logic, the state and the API.","Both methodologies help you build scalable, maintainable projects, but they do so from different points of view.\\n\\nAtomic Design (by Brad Frost)\\n- The philosophy: decomposing the user interface into small, reusable components, in the way that matter is made up of atoms, molecules and so on.\\n- The levels:\\n- Atoms: the basic building blocks (buttons, inputs, icons).\\n- Molecules: simple combinations of atoms (a search bar = an input field + a button).\\n- Organisms: more complex, complete blocks of the interface (a header containing the logo, the navigation and the search molecule).\\n- Templates: page layouts without content, showing the structure.\\n- Pages: specific instances of the templates with real content.\\n\\n- The focus: first and foremost on the visual side and on the reuse of components.\\n\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD)\\n- The philosophy: organizing the whole codebase by business capability (features) and separating the responsibilities between the various architectural layers strictly.\\n- The layers (from the low to the high level):\\n- Shared: reusable shared modules (the UI kit, the utilities, the API instances).\\n- Entities: the business entities (`User`, `Product`, `Order`, for example).\\n- Features: the business capabilities specific to the application (`auth-by-email`, `product-search`, for example). Each feature contains all of the logic it needs in order to work (the UI, the hooks, the store, the API calls).\\n- Widgets: composite blocks that assemble several features and entities in order to build complex sections of pages.\\n- Pages: the application\'s final pages, composite layers that assemble the widgets and the features.\\n- App: the application\'s global configuration, the providers, the styles.\\n\\n- The focus: on the architecture of the whole application, on the separation of responsibilities and on keeping the business logic manageable.\\n\\nThe key differences:\\n- Scope: Atomic Design is a design system and a component structure. FSD is a full architectural methodology for a whole project.\\n- A hybrid approach: these methodologies are often used together. Within FSD\'s `shared/ui` layer you can organize the components on Atomic Design principles, and in the `features` and `pages` layers use those components in order to build the business logic.",null],[2128,"Material UI (MUI) is a comprehensive component library implementing Google\'s Material Design guidelines; it provides ready-made, styled, functional components out of the box. ShadCN/ui is not a library in the traditional sense but a collection of accessible, configurable components whose code you copy into your own project and control entirely; it is built on Tailwind CSS and Radix UI. NDesign (now known as Gravity UI) is a design system from Nordeck, often used in corporate products.","The choice of UI kit depends on the project\'s requirements: whether you need maximum development speed with ready-made components or full control over the appearance and the code.\\n\\nMaterial UI (MUI)\\n- Description: the most popular React component library. It provides an enormous number of ready-made, interactive, well-tested components (buttons, tables, dialogs, menus).\\n- Characteristics:\\n- It follows the principles of Material Design.\\n- Deep customization through the theme (ThemeProvider) or through styling with the `sx` prop.\\n- It has its own styling system (Emotion).\\n- Very complete documentation and a large community.\\n\\n- It is ideal for: a fast start and for projects where the Material Design style is acceptable or where there are resources for customizing it deeply.\\n\\nShadCN/ui\\n- Description: this is not a library installed through npm. It is a set of code for components that you copy into your own project by means of a CLI utility.\\n- Characteristics:\\n- It is built on Radix UI\'s primitives (which provide the accessibility and the interaction logic) and styled by means of Tailwind CSS (which provides the appearance).\\n- You get full control over a component\'s code and can change it easily.\\n- Out of the box the components look modern and minimalist.\\n- Excellent accessibility (a11y).\\n\\n- It is ideal for: projects where full control over the UI matters, where Tailwind CSS is used and where you are prepared to manage the components\' code a little more.\\n\\nAnt Design / NDesign (Gravity UI)\\n- Description: another powerful component library, very popular in the enterprise segment and in the React ecosystem. NDesign (Gravity UI) is less widespread but a good design system.\\n- Characteristics:\\n- It offers a professional, \\"corporate\\" look.\\n- A rich set of complex components (advanced tables, for example).\\n- It is often used in admin panels and internal tools.",null],[2129,"The main approaches: WebSockets for a permanent two-way connection between the client and the server, which lets the server push data instantly. SSE (Server-Sent Events) for a one-way stream of data from the server to the client, which is simpler but less powerful. Long polling as a fallback approach, where the client polls the server constantly, waiting for new data. And GraphQL Subscriptions as a specialized protocol on top of WebSockets for queries to a GraphQL API.","To create \\"live\\" interfaces (chats, notifications, stock tickers, dashboards) various technologies are used, each with its own pros and cons.\\n\\n1. WebSockets (`ws://`, `wss://`)\\n- How it works: after an initial HTTP handshake, a permanent full-duplex (two-way) TCP connection is established. The server and the client can exchange data at any moment, independently of one another.\\n- The pros: minimal latency, low overhead, two-way communication.\\n- The cons: harder to implement and to maintain on the server.\\n- The libraries: `Socket.IO` (the most popular; it provides fallbacks), `ws` (for Node.js).\\n\\n2. Server-Sent Events (SSE)\\n- How it works: a technology that lets the server send a stream of events to the client over an ordinary HTTP connection. The client connects to an endpoint and listens to the stream of data.\\n- The pros: a very simple API on the client (through `EventSource`); it works over HTTP and requires no special server protocol.\\n- The cons: one-way communication only (server -> client). There are limitations (there is no support for authorization through headers in its pure form without workarounds, for example).\\n- It is ideal for: notifications, news feeds, broadcasting data.\\n\\n3. Long polling\\n- How it works: an obsolete but reliable method. The client sends a request to the server. The server holds the request open until new data appears, and then responds. The client sends a new request immediately after receiving the response.\\n- The pros: it works everywhere HTTP works.\\n- The cons: high latency (waiting for the response), a load on the server because of the large number of hanging connections.\\n- It is used nowadays: mainly as a fallback for older browsers or when the other technologies are unavailable.\\n\\n4. GraphQL Subscriptions\\n- How it works: GraphQL\'s specification for working in real time. It normally uses WebSockets as its transport (most often over the `graphql-ws` protocol). The client \\"subscribes\\" to an event, and the server sends a notification when it occurs.\\n- The pros: integration with the GraphQL ecosystem, type safety, a declarative approach.\\n- The cons: it ties you to using GraphQL on the backend.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of technology depends on the task. WebSockets — for full interactivity (chats, games). SSE — for simply broadcasting data. Long polling — as a last resort. GraphQL Subscriptions — for projects that already use GraphQL.",null],[2130,"A trading terminal needs a low-latency architecture: a React frontend with WebSocket connections, a backend of microservices with horizontal scaling, caching in Redis for fast access to the data, time series in ClickHouse for analytics and PostgreSQL for the main operations. Particular attention goes to the geographical distribution of the servers close to the exchanges, to data replication and to fault tolerance.","Designing a trading platform calls for particular attention to performance, reliability and low latency.\\n\\nThe architectural components:\\n\\nThe frontend:\\n- React with virtualization in order to display large volumes of data\\n- WebSockets for real-time quotes\\n- Local caching of the data used most often\\n\\nThe backend services:\\n- An API Gateway for routing the requests\\n- A quotes microservice with WebSocket connections\\n- An orders service for handling the trading operations\\n- An analytics service for computing the indicators\\n\\nThe databases:\\n- PostgreSQL for the main operations and the user data\\n- ClickHouse for storing time series and for analytics\\n- Redis for caching and for sessions\\n\\nAn example of how the microservices are organized:\\n\\nThe critical aspects:\\n\\nPerformance:\\n- Placing the servers geographically close to the exchanges\\n- The use of UDP for high-frequency data\\n- Optimizing the network infrastructure\\n\\nReliability:\\n- Replicating the data between data centres\\n- Automatic failover\\n- Real-time backups\\n\\nSecurity:\\n- Encrypting the data in transit and at rest\\n- Two-factor authentication\\n- Auditing every operation","// The quotes microservice\\nconst WebSocket = require(\'ws\');\\nconst wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });\\n\\nwss.on(\'connection\', function connection(ws) {\\n  // Sending real quote data\\n  const interval = setInterval(() => {\\n    ws.send(JSON.stringify({\\n      symbol: \'EUR/USD\',\\n      bid: 1.0850,\\n      ask: 1.0852,\\n      timestamp: Date.now()\\n    }));\\n  }, 100);\\n});"],[2131,"To keep control of dependency versions, use lock files (package-lock.json), CI/CD checks, tools such as Renovate for automatic updates, and monorepos with a single configuration. Clear rules within the team, code review and automated compatibility tests on the versions also help.","Ensuring that dependencies are consistent across micro-frontends calls for a combination of technical solutions and processes.\\n\\nThe technical solutions:\\n\\nLock files:\\n- The mandatory use of package-lock.json or yarn.lock\\n- Committing the lock files to the repository\\n- A ban on changing versions in the lock files by hand\\n\\nAutomation tools:\\n- Renovate or Dependabot for automatic updates\\n- npm audit for checking for vulnerabilities\\n- CI/CD pipelines with version checks\\n\\nAn example of a Renovate configuration:\\n\\nThe organizational measures:\\n\\nThe development processes:\\n- Shared rules for updating dependencies\\n- Mandatory code review of changes to package.json\\n- Regular synchronization between the teams\\n\\nThe architectural approaches:\\n- A monorepo with single versions of the dependencies\\n- Shared libraries for the common components\\n- Containerization in order to isolate the environments\\n\\nMonitoring and control:\\n- Automated checks in CI/CD\\n- Notifications about incompatible versions\\n- Dashboards with current information about the dependencies","{\\n  \\"extends\\": [\\n    \\"config:base\\",\\n    \\":dependencyDashboard\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\"packageRules\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"matchUpdateTypes\\": [\\"minor\\", \\"patch\\"],\\n      \\"automerge\\": true\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"matchDepTypes\\": [\\"devDependencies\\"],\\n      \\"automerge\\": true\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\"rangeStrategy\\": \\"bump\\"\\n}"],[2132,"In React 17 you can use `ReactDOM.unstable_batchedUpdates()` in order to group several state updates into a single re-render. In some cases automatic batching also works in React\'s event handlers. In React 18 batching works automatically in every scenario, asynchronous operations included.","Batching state updates lets you cut down the number of re-renders and improve performance.\\n\\nThe methods of batching in React 17:\\n\\n1. unstable_batchedUpdates:\\n\\n2. Automatic batching in event handlers:\\n\\n3. The problems with asynchronous code:\\n\\nReact 18\'s improvements:\\n\\nAutomatic batching:\\n\\nCustom implementations:","import { unstable_batchedUpdates } from \'react-dom\';\\n\\n// Grouping several setState calls into a single re-render\\nunstable_batchedUpdates(() => {\\n  setCount(count + 1);\\n  setName(\'New Name\');\\n  setLoading(false);\\n});\\n\\n// React batches updates in synchronous handlers automatically\\nconst handleClick = () => {\\n  setCount(count + 1);    // These three updates\\n  setName(\'New Name\');    // will be grouped\\n  setLoading(false);      // into a single re-render\\n};\\n\\n// WITHOUT batching - several re-renders\\nfetch(\'/api/data\').then(data => {\\n  setLoading(false);      // Re-render 1\\n  setData(data);          // Re-render 2\\n  setError(null);         // Re-render 3\\n});\\n\\n// With batching - a single re-render\\nfetch(\'/api/data\').then(data => {\\n  unstable_batchedUpdates(() => {\\n    setLoading(false);\\n    setData(data);\\n    setError(null);\\n  });\\n});\\n\\n// In React 18 every update is batched automatically\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  setCount(count + 1);    // All three updates\\n  setName(\'New Name\');    // will be grouped\\n  setLoading(false);      // automatically\\n}, 1000);\\n\\n// A hook for batching in React 17\\nconst useBatchedUpdates = () => {\\n  const batchUpdates = (callback) => {\\n    if (unstable_batchedUpdates) {\\n      unstable_batchedUpdates(callback);\\n    } else {\\n      callback();\\n    }\\n  };\\n  return batchUpdates;\\n};"],[2133,"Jest works without a browser thanks to jsdom — a JavaScript implementation of the browser APIs, which emulates the DOM, events and other web standards in a Node.js environment. That lets you run component tests in an isolated environment with full control over the runtime and with fast test start-up.","Testing React components in Jest is possible thanks to a combination of several technologies.\\n\\nThe testing architecture:\\n\\n1. jsdom — a browser emulator:\\n- An implementation of the DOM API in Node.js\\n- Support for events, selectors and navigation\\n- An isolated environment for each test\\n\\n2. React\'s virtual DOM:\\n- React tests components in memory\\n- No real painting in a browser is required\\n- Fast creation and comparison of component trees\\n\\nAn example of a test:\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach:\\n\\nSpeed:\\n- No browser overhead\\n- Tests run in parallel\\n- Fast initialization of the environment\\n\\nReliability:\\n- A predictable runtime environment\\n- No side effects between tests\\n- Ease of debugging\\n\\njsdom\'s functionality:\\n\\nThe limitations:\\n- There is no real painting on the screen\\n- The Web APIs are limited\\n- There is no visual check","import { render, screen } from \'@testing-library/react\';\\nimport userEvent from \'@testing-library/user-event\';\\nimport Button from \'./Button\';\\n\\ntest(\'button click calls onClick handler\', () => {\\n  const handleClick = jest.fn();\\n  \\n  // Rendering into jsdom\\n  render(<Button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</Button>);\\n  \\n  // Interacting through the emulated events\\n  userEvent.click(screen.getByText(\'Click me\'));\\n  \\n  // Checking the results\\n  expect(handleClick).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\\n});\\n\\n// jsdom provides the browser APIs\\nglobal.document; // Access to the DOM\\nglobal.window;   // The window object\\nglobal.navigator;// The navigator (limited)\\nglobal.location; // The location object"],[2134,"The main differences between Jest with jsdom and a real browser include: the absence or the limited implementation of many Web APIs (clipboard, geolocation, media queries), no real painting on the screen, limited CSS support, and no real network requests or navigation. The performance, the debugging capabilities and the error handling differ too.","Although jsdom provides a good emulation of a browser, there are significant differences from a real runtime environment.\\n\\nThe key differences:\\n\\n1. Missing or limited Web APIs:\\n\\nThe Clipboard API:\\n\\nThe Geolocation API:\\n\\n2. The rendering limitations:\\n\\nCSS and styles:\\n- Limited CSSOM support\\n- Media queries do not work\\n- There are no real style computations\\n- There is no visual check\\n\\nSizes and layout:\\n\\n3. The network capabilities:\\n\\nReal vs emulated requests:\\n\\n4. Performance and behaviour:\\n\\nTimers and animation:\\n- `requestAnimationFrame` works differently\\n- Timers can be \\"faked\\" with `jest.useFakeTimers()`\\n- There is no real GPU acceleration\\n\\nAn example of handling the differences:","// In a real browser\\nawait navigator.clipboard.writeText(\'text\');\\n\\n// In Jest/jsdom - it does not work\\n// Mocking is required\\nObject.assign(navigator, {\\n  clipboard: {\\n    writeText: jest.fn(),\\n  },\\n});\\n\\n// Mocking for the tests\\nconst mockGeolocation = {\\n  getCurrentPosition: jest.fn(),\\n  watchPosition: jest.fn(),\\n};\\nglobal.navigator.geolocation = mockGeolocation;\\n\\n// In jsdom many properties return 0\\nelement.getBoundingClientRect(); // {x: 0, y: 0, width: 0, height: 0}\\n\\n// Mocking is required\\nElement.prototype.getBoundingClientRect = jest.fn(() => ({\\n  width: 100,\\n  height: 50,\\n  top: 0,\\n  left: 0,\\n  bottom: 50,\\n  right: 100,\\n}));\\n\\n// Jest uses mocks for fetch/XMLHttpRequest\\nglobal.fetch = jest.fn();\\nfetch.mockResolvedValue({\\n  json: () => Promise.resolve({ data: \'test\' })\\n});\\n\\n// Adapting the tests to jsdom\'s limitations\\nbeforeEach(() => {\\n  // Mocking the missing APIs\\n  Object.defineProperty(window, \'matchMedia\', {\\n    writable: true,\\n    value: jest.fn().mockImplementation(query => ({\\n      matches: false,\\n      media: query,\\n      onchange: null,\\n      addListener: jest.fn(),\\n      removeListener: jest.fn(),\\n      addEventListener: jest.fn(),\\n      removeEventListener: jest.fn(),\\n      dispatchEvent: jest.fn(),\\n    })),\\n  });\\n});"],[2135,"To force a page to reload, use `location.reload()`, which reloads the current page. For a full reload with the cache cleared, use `location.reload(true)`. You can also set `location.href = location.href` or use `location.replace()` in order to replace the current page.","There are several methods of reloading a page, each with its own characteristics.\\n\\nThe reloading methods:\\n\\n1. location.reload():\\n\\n2. Resetting the URL:\\n\\n3. The navigation methods:\\n\\nThe differences between the methods:\\n\\nlocation.reload() vs location.href:\\n- `reload()` always makes a request to the server\\n- `location.href` may take the page from the cache\\n- `reload(true)` guarantees a fresh version\\n\\nAn example with handling:\\n\\nThe special cases:\\n\\nReloading with parameters:\\n\\nSPA (Single Page Application):\\n\\nRecommendations:\\n- Use `location.reload()` for most cases\\n- Add a confirmation for the user\\n- In an SPA, prefer updating the data without a reload","// A simple reload\\nlocation.reload();\\n\\n// A forced reload with the cache cleared\\nlocation.reload(true);\\n\\n// The effect is similar to reload()\\nlocation.href = location.href;\\n\\n// Without adding to the browser\'s history\\nlocation.replace(location.href);\\n\\n// Using the history API\\nhistory.go(0);\\n\\n// The same as reload()\\nhistory.go();\\n\\nfunction forceReload() {\\n  try {\\n    // We try to force a reload\\n    location.reload(true);\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    // A fallback for older browsers\\n    location.href = location.href + \'?force=\' + Date.now();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A reload with confirmation\\nfunction confirmReload() {\\n  if (confirm(\'Reload the page?\')) {\\n    location.reload();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Adding a timestamp in order to avoid caching\\nlocation.search = \'?refresh=\' + Date.now();\\n\\n// In an SPA, instead of a full reload\\nwindow.dispatchEvent(new Event(\'forceRefresh\'));\\n// The components handle the event and update their data"],[2136,"I will start by profiling the performance with the React DevTools, then split the component into smaller ones, use React.memo in order to prevent unnecessary re-renders, implement virtualization for the large list, lazy loading for the images and handling for the broken ones. I will also check the memoization of the computations and optimize the event handlers.","Optimizing a large component calls for a systematic approach and the use of specialized tools.\\n\\nThe optimization plan:\\n\\n1. Analysis and profiling:\\n\\n2. Splitting the component up:\\n\\n3. Optimizing the re-renders:\\n\\n4. Virtualizing the list:\\n\\n5. Optimizing the images:\\n\\n6. Handling the broken images:\\n\\nThe tools for diagnosis:\\n- The React DevTools Profiler\\n- Chrome\'s Performance tab\\n- A Lighthouse audit\\n- why-did-you-render for debugging re-renders\\n\\nThe optimization priorities:\\n- Eliminating the bottlenecks\\n- Reducing the number of re-renders\\n- Optimizing the large lists\\n- Lazy loading of the content\\n- Caching the computations","// The React DevTools Profiler\\n// - Recording interactions\\n// - Finding \\"expensive\\" components\\n// - Analysing the render times\\n\\n// Chrome DevTools\' Performance tab\\n// - Record performance\\n// - Identify bottlenecks\\n// - Memory leaks detection\\n\\n// Instead of one large component\\nconst BigComponent = () => {\\n  // 1000 lines of code\\n};\\n\\n// Split into logical parts\\nconst OptimizedComponent = () => (\\n  <>\\n    <Gallery images={images} />\\n    <ItemList items={items} />\\n    <UserPanel user={user} />\\n  </>\\n);\\n\\n// React.memo in order to prevent unnecessary renders\\nconst Gallery = React.memo(({ images }) => {\\n  return images.map(image => (\\n    <Image key={image.id} src={image.url} />\\n  ));\\n});\\n\\n// useMemo for heavy computations\\nconst processedItems = useMemo(() => {\\n  return items.map(heavyTransformation);\\n}, [items]);\\n\\n// useCallback for stable references\\nconst handleClick = useCallback((id) => {\\n  // handling the click\\n}, []);\\n\\n// Using react-window or react-virtualized\\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from \'react-window\';\\n\\nconst VirtualizedList = ({ items }) => (\\n  <List\\n    height={400}\\n    itemCount={items.length}\\n    itemSize={50}\\n  >\\n    {({ index, style }) => (\\n      <div style={style}>\\n        {items[index].name}\\n      </div>\\n    )}\\n  </List>\\n);\\n\\n// Lazy loading with an Intersection Observer\\nconst LazyImage = ({ src, alt }) => {\\n  const [isVisible, setIsVisible] = useState(false);\\n  const imgRef = useRef();\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(([entry]) => {\\n      setIsVisible(entry.isIntersecting);\\n    });\\n    observer.observe(imgRef.current);\\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div ref={imgRef}>\\n      {isVisible && <img src={src} alt={alt} />}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nconst ImageWithFallback = ({ src, alt, fallback }) => {\\n  const [hasError, setHasError] = useState(false);\\n  const [currentSrc, setCurrentSrc] = useState(src);\\n\\n  const handleError = () => {\\n    if (!hasError) {\\n      setHasError(true);\\n      setCurrentSrc(fallback);\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <img \\n      src={currentSrc} \\n      alt={alt}\\n      onError={handleError}\\n      loading=\\"lazy\\"\\n    />\\n  );\\n};"],[2137,"To handle broken images, use the `onError` event on the `<img>` element in order to replace the src with a fallback image, to set an error state in a React component, or to hide/replace the element. You can also use CSS to show a placeholder and preload the critically important images.","Handling broken images matters to the user experience and to the application\'s reliability.\\n\\nThe methods of handling them:\\n\\n1. The basic JavaScript approach:\\n\\n2. A React component with error handling:\\n\\n3. A custom hook for images:\\n\\n4. Multiple fallbacks:\\n\\n5. The CSS-based approach:\\n\\n6. Preloading and caching:\\n\\nThe best practices:\\n- Always provide alt text\\n- Use lazy loading for the non-critical images\\n- Have multiple fallback strategies\\n- Monitor image-loading errors","// Handling it in plain JS\\nconst img = new Image();\\nimg.src = \'image.jpg\';\\nimg.onerror = function() {\\n  this.src = \'/fallback.jpg\';\\n  this.alt = \'The image was not found\';\\n};\\n\\nconst ImageWithFallback = ({ src, alt, fallback }) => {\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(false);\\n\\n  const handleError = () => {\\n    setError(true);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <img\\n      src={error ? fallback : src}\\n      alt={alt}\\n      onError={handleError}\\n    />\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Usage\\n<ImageWithFallback \\n  src=\\"/possible-broken-image.jpg\\"\\n  fallback=\\"/default-image.jpg\\"\\n  alt=\\"A description\\"\\n/>\\n\\nconst useImageLoader = (src) => {\\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState(\'loading\');\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const img = new Image();\\n    img.src = src;\\n    \\n    img.onload = () => setStatus(\'loaded\');\\n    img.onerror = () => setStatus(\'error\');\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      img.onload = null;\\n      img.onerror = null;\\n    };\\n  }, [src]);\\n\\n  return status;\\n};\\n\\n// Using it in a component\\nconst ImageComponent = ({ src }) => {\\n  const status = useImageLoader(src);\\n\\n  if (status === \'loading\') {\\n    return <div className=\\"image-placeholder\\">Loading...</div>;\\n  }\\n\\n  if (status === \'error\') {\\n    return <img src=\\"/fallback.jpg\\" alt=\\"The fallback image\\" />;\\n  }\\n\\n  return <img src={src} alt=\\"The main image\\" />;\\n};\\n\\nconst RobustImage = ({ src, alt, fallbacks = [] }) => {\\n  const [currentIndex, setCurrentIndex] = useState(0);\\n  const sources = [src, ...fallbacks];\\n\\n  const handleError = () => {\\n    if (currentIndex < sources.length - 1) {\\n      setCurrentIndex(currentIndex + 1);\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <img\\n      src={sources[currentIndex]}\\n      alt={alt}\\n      onError={handleError}\\n    />\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Usage with several fallback options\\n<RobustImage\\n  src=\\"/primary-image.jpg\\"\\n  fallbacks={[\\n    \'/secondary-image.jpg\',\\n    \'/tertiary-image.png\',\\n    \'/final-fallback.jpg\'\\n  ]}\\n  alt=\\"A very robust image\\"\\n/>\\n\\n.image-container {\\n  position: relative;\\n  width: 100%;\\n  height: 200px;\\n  background-color: #f0f0f0;\\n}\\n\\n.image-container img {\\n  width: 100%;\\n  height: 100%;\\n  object-fit: cover;\\n}\\n\\n.image-container img:error {\\n  display: none;\\n}\\n\\n.image-container:has(img:error)::after {\\n  content: \'The image is not available\';\\n  display: flex;\\n  align-items: center;\\n  justify-content: center;\\n  height: 100%;\\n  color: #666;\\n}\\n\\n// Preloading the critical images\\nconst preloadImages = (urls) => {\\n  urls.forEach(url => {\\n    const img = new Image();\\n    img.src = url;\\n  });\\n};\\n\\n// Usage\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  preloadImages([\\n    \'/critical-image1.jpg\',\\n    \'/critical-image2.jpg\',\\n    \'/fallback-image.jpg\'\\n  ]);\\n}, []);"],[2138,"Hooks are functions that let you use state and React\'s other capabilities in functional components. The rules for calling them: 1) Call hooks only at the top level, not inside loops, conditionals or nested functions. 2) Call hooks only from React functions (functional components or other custom hooks). These rules are needed so that React can match up and keep each hook\'s state correctly across the multiple `useState` and `useEffect` calls on every render.","Hooks revolutionized React by letting you use state and the lifecycle in functional components. Their internal implementation nevertheless imposes strict rules.\\n\\nWhat are hooks for?\\n\\n- Using state: `useState` lets a functional component hold and update internal state.\\n- Side effects: `useEffect` lets you run code that interacts with the outside world (API requests, subscriptions) after the component has rendered.\\n- Reusing logic: custom hooks let you extract and reuse stateful logic between different components.\\n\\nThe rules for calling hooks\\nReact relies on the order in which hooks are called in order to match each hook up with its state correctly. Breaking that order breaks the mechanism.\\n- Call hooks only at the top level\\n- They must not be called inside conditionals, loops or nested functions.\\n- Why: React uses the order in which hooks are called in order to determine which state belongs to which component. If an `if` condition skips a hook, the order is thrown off and the subsequent hooks will get the wrong state.\\n\\n- Call hooks only from React functions\\n- You may: inside functional components.\\n- You may: inside other custom hooks.\\n- You may not: in ordinary JavaScript functions or in class components.\\n\\nConclusion: keeping to these rules strictly guarantees that the state and the effects in your components will work predictably and without bugs. The ESLint plugin `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` checks that the rules are observed automatically.","// WRONG\\nif (someCondition) {\\n  const [state, setState] = useState(); // The hook may not be called\\n}\\n\\n// RIGHT\\nconst [state, setState] = useState();\\nif (someCondition) {\\n  // Use state here\\n}"],[2139,"When a parent component\'s `key` changes, React interprets that as meaning this is a completely new component. The old instance of the component and all of its children will be unmounted entirely (the clean-up functions in `useEffect` will be called), and then a completely new instance with clean state will be mounted. This is a powerful tool for forcing a component\'s state to be reset.","A `key` is a special prop that helps React determine which elements in a list have changed, been added or been removed. It also plays a role in identifying components.\\n\\nThe process when a key changes\\nWhen React encounters a component whose `key` has changed, it starts the following process:\\n- Unmounting:\\n- React destroys the current instance of the component and all of its descendants entirely.\\n- The clean-up functions in every `useEffect` hook are run (the value returned from `useEffect`).\\n- The component\'s local state (created through `useState`, `useRef`) is lost irretrievably.\\n\\n- Mounting:\\n- React creates a completely new instance of the component, \\"from a clean slate\\".\\n- A new, initial state is initialized.\\n- The component and all of its child elements are rendered.\\n- The `useEffect` hooks are called after mounting.\\n\\nA practical example\\nImagine a counter component whose state has to be reset when the user is switched.\\n\\n- If `currentUser` changes from `{id: 1, name: \'Alice\'}` to `{id: 2, name: \'Bob\'}`, the `key` changes from `1` to `2`.\\n- The component for Alice will unmount (the `messageCount` state will be reset, and the cleanup in `useEffect` will run), and then a new component for Bob will mount with `messageCount = 0`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nChanging the `key` is a signal to React that the element/component is to be treated as new, which leads to it and all of its children being recreated entirely. This is useful for forcing internal state to be reset or side effects to be restarted.","// UserProfile receives different users and a key equal to user.id\\nfunction UserProfile({ user }) {\\n  const [messageCount, setMessageCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The effect for loading the user\'s data\\n    console.log(\'Mounting or updating UserProfile for\', user.name);\\n    return () => {\\n      console.log(\'Unmounting UserProfile for\', user.name); // The clean-up\\n    };\\n  }, [user.name]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h2>{user.name}</h2>\\n      <p>Messages: {messageCount}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setMessageCount(c => c + 1)}>+1</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Usage:\\n// <UserProfile key={currentUser.id} user={currentUser} />"],[2140,"To avoid an application failing completely because of an error in a child component, use Error Boundaries. These are special React components that catch JavaScript errors in any of their child components, log those errors and display a fallback UI instead of the \\"broken\\" subtree of components. Error Boundaries do not catch errors inside themselves, in asynchronous code or in event handlers.","Before Error Boundaries appeared, any error during rendering led to a \\"white screen\\" across the whole application.\\n\\nWhat is an Error Boundary?\\nIt is a class component that implements at least one of two lifecycle methods:\\n- `static getDerivedStateFromError(error)` — used to render a fallback UI after an error has occurred.\\n- `componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo)` — used to log information about the error.\\n\\nHow do you use it?\\n\\n- Create an error-boundary component:\\n\\n- Wrap the problem parts of the application in it:\\n\\nConclusion\\nPlacing Error Boundaries strategically at the key points of your application (around routes, navigation bars or third-party widgets, for example) lets you isolate failures and prevent the user interface from crashing completely, giving the user an informative message instead of a \\"white screen\\".","class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.state = { hasError: false };\\n  }\\n\\n  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {\\n    // Update the state so that the next render shows the fallback UI.\\n    return { hasError: true };\\n  }\\n\\n  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {\\n    // Log the error to a reporting service (Sentry, for example)\\n    console.error(\'Error caught by boundary:\', error, errorInfo);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    if (this.state.hasError) {\\n      // You can render any fallback UI\\n      return this.props.fallback || <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;\\n    }\\n    return this.props.children;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// You can wrap the whole application\\n<ErrorBoundary fallback={<p>Oops! An error occurred.</p>}>\\n  <App />\\n</ErrorBoundary>\\n\\n// Or wrap just one particular unreliable widget\\n<ErrorBoundary>\\n  <BuggyComponent />\\n</ErrorBoundary>"],[2141,"A key is a special string attribute in React that helps identify the elements of a list. When React updates a list, it uses the keys in order to determine which elements have been changed, added or removed. That lets React repaint only the elements that have changed efficiently instead of updating the whole list. Without keys React may determine the changes incorrectly, which will lead to display bugs and reduced performance.","Keys are a fundamental concept in React for working with lists of elements and for optimizing the re-rendering process.\\n\\nWhat keys are for\\n\\n- Identifying elements — uniquely determining each element of a list\\n- Optimizing performance — preventing unnecessary re-renders\\n- Preserving state — updating the state of the components in a list correctly\\n\\nHow keys work\\n\\nWithout keys\\n\\nWith keys\\n\\nThe rules for using keys\\n\\n- Keys must be unique among sibling elements\\n- Keys must not change between renders\\n- Do not use the array index if the order of the elements can change\\n\\nAn example of the problem without keys\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use unique identifiers from your data (id, slug and so on)\\n- Avoid indexes when the data can change\\n- Generate stable keys if there are no natural identifiers\\n\\nConclusion: keys are critically important to lists working correctly in React. They ensure that the interface is updated properly, that the components\' state is preserved and that performance is optimized when working with dynamic data.","const list = items.map(item => (\\n    <li>{item.name}</li>  // Warning: Each child should have a key\\n));\\n\\nconst list = items.map(item => (\\n    <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>  // The correct usage\\n));\\n\\n// The initial state\\nconst items = [\'Apple\', \'Banana\', \'Orange\'];\\n\\n// After deleting \'Apple\'\\nconst newItems = [\'Banana\', \'Orange\'];\\n\\n// Without keys React may update the DOM incorrectly"],[2142,"When the elements have no ready-made ids, several strategies can be used to create keys. If the order of the elements is guaranteed not to change, the array index can be used as a temporary measure. For dynamic data it is better to generate unique keys from an element\'s content or to use special libraries for generating UUIDs. You can also combine several data fields in order to create a composite key. The main thing is to ensure that the keys are stable between renders.","The absence of unique identifiers on the elements of a list is a common situation, and there are various solutions for it.\\n\\nThe strategies for generating keys\\n\\n1. The array index (limited use)\\n\\nThe limitations:\\n- It does not work when the order of the elements changes\\n- It can cause problems with the components\' state\\n\\n2. Generating them from the content\\n\\n3. Using external libraries\\n\\nThe practical approaches\\n\\nComposite keys\\n\\nGenerating the keys on the server\\n\\nRecommendations on choosing a strategy\\n\\n- Static data — use a combination of fields or generate them on load\\n- Dynamic data — generate the keys when the elements are created\\n- Server data — ask the server for identifiers\\n\\nAn example implementation\\n\\nConclusion: when there are no ready-made ids it is important to choose a key-generation strategy that ensures uniqueness and stability between renders. Avoid array indexes for dynamic data, and prefer meaningful composite keys or specialized solutions for generating identifiers.","// Only if the order does not change and there is no other option\\nconst list = items.map((item, index) => (\\n    <li key={index}>{item.name}</li>\\n));\\n\\nconst list = items.map(item => (\\n    <li key={`${item.name}-${item.category}`}>\\n        {item.name}\\n    </li>\\n));\\n\\nimport { v4 as uuidv4 } from \'uuid\';\\n\\nconst list = items.map(item => (\\n    <li key={uuidv4()}>{item.name}</li>\\n));\\n\\n// A combination of several fields\\nconst getKey = (item) => {\\n    return `${item.type}-${item.timestamp}-${item.author}`;\\n};\\n\\nconst list = items.map(item => (\\n    <div key={getKey(item)}>{item.content}</div>\\n));\\n\\n// If the data comes from the server, an id can be added there\\nconst list = apiData.map(item => (\\n    <div key={item.generatedId}>{item.content}</div>\\n));\\n\\nfunction ItemList({ items }) {\\n    // Generating stable keys from the content\\n    const list = items.map(item => {\\n        const key = `${item.type}-${item.createdAt}-${item.content.hash}`;\\n        return <div key={key}>{item.content}</div>;\\n    });\\n    \\n    return <div>{list}</div>;\\n}"],[2143,"\\"The staticness of an array\\" means that the order, the number and the composition of the elements in the array never change after the initial render. If the array is guaranteed to stay unchanged — no elements are added, removed or reordered — then the array index can be used as a key. In real applications such situations are rare, however, so it is better always to use unique identifiers from the data.","The staticness of an array is an important notion that determines whether indexes can safely be used as keys in React.\\n\\nWhat a static array is\\nA static array has the following characteristics:\\n- An unchanging order — the elements always stay in the same sequence\\n- A constant number — elements are not added or removed\\n- A stable composition — the elements themselves are not replaced by others\\n\\nWhen an array can be considered static\\n\\nAn example of a static array\\n\\nAn example of a NON-static array\\n\\nThe problems with using indexes in dynamic arrays\\n\\n1. State updated incorrectly\\n\\n2. Performance problems\\n\\n- Unnecessary re-renders — React cannot determine which elements have changed properly\\n- Incorrect animations — the elements may animate wrongly\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Always prefer unique ids from the data\\n- Use indexes only for data that really is static\\n- Generate keys if there are no natural identifiers\\n\\nHow to check whether an array is static\\nAn array can be considered static if:\\n- It is defined as a constant and does not change\\n- It contains a fixed set of elements (the days of the week, the months, the colours of the rainbow)\\n- It does not depend on the component\'s props or state\\n\\nConclusion: use indexes as keys only for arrays that really are static and are guaranteed not to change. For dynamic data always use unique identifiers from the data or generate stable keys.","const MONTHS = [\'January\', \'February\', \'March\', \'April\', \'May\', \'June\', \\n               \'July\', \'August\', \'September\', \'October\', \'November\', \'December\'];\\n\\nfunction MonthList() {\\n    return (\\n        <ul>\\n            {MONTHS.map((month, index) => (\\n                <li key={index}>{month}</li>  // Safe, since the array is static\\n            ))}\\n        </ul>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nfunction TodoList({ todos }) {\\n    // DANGEROUS: the array is dynamic; elements can be added/removed\\n    return (\\n        <ul>\\n            {todos.map((todo, index) => (\\n                <li key={index}>{todo.text}</li>  // It can cause problems\\n            ))}\\n        </ul>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\n// The initial state: [\'Task 1\', \'Task 2\', \'Task 3\']\\n// We delete the first element\\n// The keys change: 0->\'Task 2\', 1->\'Task 3\'\\n// React may keep the state for the wrong elements"],[2144,"Optimizing re-renders in React is achieved through several main approaches: using React.memo to memoize components, applying useMemo and useCallback to cache values and functions, managing state properly by means of useReducer, splitting components into smaller parts, and optimizing context through memoization. It is also important to avoid creating new objects and functions in a component\'s body, since that leads to unnecessary re-renders.","Optimizing the number of re-renders is a key aspect of React applications\' performance.\\n\\nThe main causes of unnecessary re-renders\\n\\n- A change of props — even if the new props have not in fact changed\\n- A change of state — an update to a component\'s state\\n- A change of context — an update to a value in the Context API\\n- A re-render of the parent — the child components are repainted automatically\\n\\nThe methods of optimization\\n\\n1. React.memo for memoizing components\\n\\n2. useMemo for caching computations\\n\\n3. useCallback for stable functions\\n\\n4. Optimizing the work with Context\\n\\n5. Splitting components up\\n\\nThe practical strategies\\n\\nAvoid creating objects in the render\\n\\nUse the right dependencies\\n\\nThe tools for debugging\\n\\n- The React DevTools Profiler — analysing components\' performance\\n- Why did you render — a library for tracking the causes of re-renders\\n\\nConclusion: optimizing re-renders calls for a combined approach — memoizing components and values, managing state properly, and separating the responsibilities between components. Begin optimizing only once you have identified real performance problems.","const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent({ data }) {\\n    // The component will re-render only when its props change\\n    return <div>{data}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Component({ items }) {\\n    const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {\\n        return items.filter(item => item.active)\\n                   .reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.value, 0);\\n    }, [items]); // It is recomputed only when items changes\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n    \\n    const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n        setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, []); // The function is not recreated on every render\\n    \\n    return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;\\n}\\n\\nconst MyContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction ContextProvider({ children }) {\\n    const [state, setState] = useState({});\\n    \\n    const value = useMemo(() => ({\\n        state,\\n        updateState: setState\\n    }), [state]);\\n    \\n    return (\\n        <MyContext.Provider value={value}>\\n            {children}\\n        </MyContext.Provider>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\n// BAD: the whole component re-renders when the input changes\\nfunction UserProfile({ user }) {\\n    const [input, setInput] = useState(\'\');\\n    \\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n            <input value={input} onChange={e => setInput(e.target.value)} />\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\n// GOOD: the input is isolated in a separate component\\nfunction UserProfile({ user }) {\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n            <UserInput />\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\n// BAD: a new object on every render\\n<Component style={{ color: \'red\' }} />\\n\\n// GOOD: moved outside the component, or useMemo\\nconst style = useMemo(() => ({ color: \'red\' }), []);\\n<Component style={style} />\\n\\n// State the minimum dependencies necessary\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n    // the effect\\n}, [dependency1, dependency2]); // Only what is really used"],[2145,"PureComponent is a base class in React that implements the `shouldComponentUpdate` method automatically with a shallow comparison of the props and the state. It prevents a component from re-rendering unnecessarily when its props and state have not changed, which improves the application\'s performance.","PureComponent in React\\nPureComponent is an optimized version of the ordinary React.Component, which handles the logic of preventing unnecessary re-renders automatically.\\n\\nHow PureComponent works\\nPureComponent implements `shouldComponentUpdate` automatically with a shallow comparison:\\n\\nA comparison with the ordinary Component\\nThe ordinary Component:\\n\\nPureComponent:\\n\\nThe mechanism of the shallow comparison\\nPureComponent compares the props and the state as follows:\\n\\nWhen to use PureComponent\\n\\nThe suitable cases:\\n\\nThe unsuitable cases:\\n\\nFunctional components with React.memo\\nFor functional components the equivalent of PureComponent is React.memo:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n1. Avoid mutations\\n\\n2. Use stable references\\n\\nConclusion: PureComponent should be used for components that receive primitive props or rarely changing objects. It prevents unnecessary re-renders automatically through its shallow comparison, but it calls for care with mutable data and with functions in the props.","class MyComponent extends React.PureComponent {\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass RegularComponent extends React.Component {\\n  // Without shouldComponentUpdate - it always re-renders\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass OptimizedComponent extends React.PureComponent {\\n  // It automatically has a shouldComponentUpdate\\n  // with a shallow comparison of the props and the state\\n  render() {\\n    return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// An example of the shallow-comparison logic\\nfunction shallowEqual(objA, objB) {\\n  if (objA === objB) return true;\\n  \\n  if (typeof objA !== \'object\' || objA === null ||\\n      typeof objB !== \'object\' || objB === null) {\\n    return false;\\n  }\\n  \\n  const keysA = Object.keys(objA);\\n  const keysB = Object.keys(objB);\\n  \\n  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;\\n  \\n  for (let i = 0; i < keysA.length; i++) {\\n    if (!objB.hasOwnProperty(keysA[i]) || objA[keysA[i]] !== objB[keysA[i]]) {\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  return true;\\n}\\n\\n// 1. Components with primitive props\\nclass UserInfo extends React.PureComponent {\\n  render() {\\n    return (\\n      <div>\\n        <h2>{this.props.name}</h2>\\n        <p>Age: {this.props.age}</p>\\n      </div>\\n    );\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 2. Static components\\nclass Header extends React.PureComponent {\\n  render() {\\n    return <header>{this.props.title}</header>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 1. Components with frequently changing objects/arrays\\nclass UserList extends React.PureComponent {\\n  render() {\\n    // Bad: users is always a new array\\n    return this.props.users.map(user => \\n      <User key={user.id} user={user} />\\n    );\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 2. Components with functions in their props\\nclass Button extends React.PureComponent {\\n  render() {\\n    // Bad: onClick is always a new function\\n    return <button onClick={this.props.onClick}>Click</button>;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(function MyComponent(props) {\\n  return <div>{props.value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\n// With a custom comparison function\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(\\n  function MyComponent(props) {\\n    return <div>{props.value}</div>;\\n  },\\n  function areEqual(prevProps, nextProps) {\\n    // It returns true if no re-render is needed\\n    return prevProps.value === nextProps.value;\\n  }\\n);\\n\\n// Bad - it creates a new object every time\\nthis.setState({ user: { ...this.state.user, name: \'A new name\' } });\\n\\n// Better - use primitives where you can\\nthis.setState({ userName: \'A new name\' });\\n\\n// Bad\\nclass Parent extends Component {\\n  handleClick = () => { /* ... */ };\\n  \\n  render() {\\n    return <Child onClick={() => {}} />;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Good\\nclass Parent extends Component {\\n  handleClick = () => { /* ... */ };\\n  \\n  render() {\\n    return <Child onClick={this.handleClick} />;\\n  }\\n}"],[2146,"The ways of optimizing React applications include: memoizing components (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback), using keys properly, code splitting, lazy loading, virtualizing lists, optimizing state, using PureComponent, and eliminating unnecessary re-renders. Optimizing the build and using profiling tools also matter.","The ways of optimizing React applications\\nOptimizing React applications covers various aspects, from the rendering of components to the loading of the application.\\n\\n1. Optimizing the rendering of components\\n\\nMemoizing components\\n\\nUsing keys properly\\n\\n2. Optimizing state\\n\\nLocalizing state\\n\\nOptimizing the structure of the state\\n\\n3. Optimizing the application\'s loading\\n\\nCode splitting and lazy loading\\n\\nPreloading components\\n\\n4. Optimizing the work with lists\\n\\nVirtualizing large lists\\n\\nPagination and infinite scroll\\n\\n5. Debugging tools and techniques\\n\\nProfiling performance\\n\\nUsing hooks for debugging\\n\\n6. Optimizing the build\\n\\nAnalysing the bundle\\n\\nRemoving unused code\\n\\nConclusion: optimizing React applications calls for a comprehensive approach, covering memoization, working with state properly, code splitting, virtualization and the use of profiling tools. Every application calls for an individual approach, depending on its specifics and its bottlenecks.","// React.memo for functional components\\nconst ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent({ data }) {\\n  return <div>{data}</div>;\\n});\\n\\n// useMemo for memoizing values\\nfunction Component({ items }) {\\n  const computedValue = useMemo(() => {\\n    return items.filter(item => item.active).length;\\n  }, [items]); // It is recomputed only when items changes\\n\\n  return <div>{computedValue}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// useCallback for memoizing functions\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  \\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }, []); // A stable reference to the function\\n\\n  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;\\n}\\n\\n// Bad - an index as the key\\n{items.map((item, index) => (\\n  <ListItem key={index} item={item} />\\n))}\\n\\n// Good - a unique ID\\n{items.map(item => (\\n  <ListItem key={item.id} item={item} />\\n))}\\n\\n// Bad - the state in the parent\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState(\'\');\\n  \\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Input value={inputValue} onChange={setInputValue} />\\n      <OtherComponent />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Good - the state in the component\\nfunction Input() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(\'\');\\n  return <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />;\\n}\\n\\n// Bad - nested objects\\nconst [user, setUser] = useState({\\n  profile: { name: \'\', age: 0 },\\n  settings: { theme: \'light\' }\\n});\\n\\n// Better - normalized state\\nconst [userName, setUserName] = useState(\'\');\\nconst [userAge, setUserAge] = useState(0);\\nconst [theme, setTheme] = useState(\'light\');\\n\\n// A dynamic import\\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(() => import(\'./LazyComponent\'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <LazyComponent />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Splitting by route\\nconst Home = lazy(() => import(\'./Home\'));\\nconst About = lazy(() => import(\'./About\'));\\n\\n// Preloading on hover\\nfunction Navigation() {\\n  const preloadAbout = () => {\\n    import(\'./About\');\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <nav>\\n      <Link to=\\"/about\\" onMouseEnter={preloadAbout}>\\n        About us\\n      </Link>\\n    </nav>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from \'react-window\';\\n\\nfunction VirtualizedList({ items }) {\\n  return (\\n    <List\\n      height={400}\\n      itemCount={items.length}\\n      itemSize={50}\\n    >\\n      {({ index, style }) => (\\n        <div style={style}>\\n          {items[index].name}\\n        </div>\\n      )}\\n    </List>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction ItemList() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\\n  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);\\n\\n  const loadMore = useCallback(() => {\\n    fetchItems(page).then(newItems => {\\n      setItems(prev => [...prev, ...newItems]);\\n      setPage(prev => prev + 1);\\n    });\\n  }, [page]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map(item => <Item key={item.id} item={item} />)}\\n      <button onClick={loadMore}>Load more</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The React DevTools Profiler\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Profiler id=\\"App\\" onRender={onRenderCallback}>\\n      <MyComponent />\\n    </Profiler>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction onRenderCallback(\\n  id,\\n  phase,\\n  actualDuration,\\n  baseDuration,\\n  startTime,\\n  commitTime\\n) {\\n  console.log(\'The rendering time:\', actualDuration);\\n}\\n\\nfunction useWhyDidYouUpdate(name, props) {\\n  const previousProps = useRef();\\n  \\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    if (previousProps.current) {\\n      const allKeys = Object.keys({ ...previousProps.current, ...props });\\n      const changes = {};\\n      \\n      allKeys.forEach(key => {\\n        if (previousProps.current[key] !== props[key]) {\\n          changes[key] = {\\n            from: previousProps.current[key],\\n            to: props[key]\\n          };\\n        }\\n      });\\n      \\n      if (Object.keys(changes).length) {\\n        console.log(\'[why-did-you-update]\', name, changes);\\n      }\\n    }\\n    \\n    previousProps.current = props;\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// package.json\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"analyze\\": \\"source-map-explorer \'build/static/js/*.js\'\\",\\n    \\"build\\": \\"react-scripts build\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Tree shaking works better with ES6 imports\\nimport { Button } from \'ui-library\'; // Good\\n// import * as UI from \'ui-library\'; // Bad for tree shaking"],[2147,"A monorepo is an approach in which several projects or packages are held in a single repository. The approach makes sharing code, synchronizing dependencies and keeping to shared standards easier. It is often used in large teams and in ecosystems with several frontend applications. A monorepo helps reduce code duplication and makes refactoring easier. It does call for more complex tools for building and for managing dependencies, however.","A monorepo is an architectural approach in which all of the related code is held in a single repository rather than in several separate ones.\\n\\nWhat problems a monorepo solves in frontend\\n\\n- Reusing code\\n- Shared UI components\\n- Hooks and utilities\\n- Shared configurations (`eslint`, `prettier`, `tsconfig`)\\n\\n- Synchronizing versions\\n- Every package uses the same versions of the libraries\\n- There are no incompatibility problems between the packages\\n\\n- Making refactoring easier\\n- You can change components\' APIs safely\\n- The changes are visible in every application at once\\n\\n- Shared development rules\\n- Shared linters\\n- A shared code style\\n- Shared CI pipelines\\n\\nWhere it is used most often\\n\\n- Large frontend applications\\n- Design systems\\n- Micro-frontend architectures\\n- Ecosystems with several SPAs\\n\\nThe possible difficulties\\n\\n- A more complex build set-up\\n- Greater demands on the tooling\\n- A longer initial setup\\n\\nConclusion: a monorepo is worth using when there are several related frontend projects and a need to reuse code and infrastructure actively.",null],[2148,"For working with a monorepo, special tools are used that help you manage the dependencies, the build and the running of the projects. The most popular of them are Nx, Turborepo and Lerna. Yarn Workspaces or pnpm Workspaces are also often used. These tools let you optimize the build and make development in large repositories easier.","To work with a monorepo effectively, a single Git repository is not enough — additional tools are needed.\\n\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- Nx\\n- It manages the dependencies between the packages\\n- It optimizes the build through caching\\n- It suits React and Next.js well\\n\\n- Turborepo\\n- A focus on build speed\\n- It uses intelligent task caching\\n- It is often used in frontend ecosystems\\n\\n- Lerna\\n- A historically popular tool\\n- It manages the packages\' versions\\n- Nowadays it is often used together with workspaces\\n\\nDependency managers with monorepo support\\n\\n- Yarn Workspaces\\n- pnpm Workspaces\\n- npm Workspaces\\n\\nThey let you:\\n- Hold the dependencies at the root level\\n- Link local packages without publishing them\\n\\nWhat is normally automated\\n\\n- The build (`build`)\\n- Running the tests\\n- Linting\\n- Deployment\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of tool depends on the scale of the project, but in modern frontend projects Nx or Turborepo in combination with workspaces is the most common choice.",null],[2149,"When an access token expires, the frontend should try to refresh it by means of the refresh token. The user should not notice the process. If the refresh succeeds, the request is repeated. If it does not, the user is logged out. This scenario gives a smooth user experience.","An access token has a short lifetime, so the frontend has to be ready for it to expire at any moment.\\n\\nThe typical behaviour of the frontend\\nWhen the access token has expired:\\n- The backend returns an error\\n- The frontend starts the token-refresh process\\n- It receives a new access token\\n- It repeats the original request\\n\\nWhat is important to bear in mind\\n\\n- The user should not see an error\\n- The process should be automatic\\n- The repeat of the request should be transparent\\n\\nIf the refresh token is invalid\\nIn that case:\\n- refreshing the access token is impossible\\n- the session counts as finished\\n- the frontend clears the authorization state\\n- the user is redirected to the login page\\n\\nThe practical aspect\\nThe frontend normally:\\n- holds a flag for the token refresh\\n- blocks parallel refresh requests\\n- avoids infinite refresh loops\\n\\nConclusion: handling the expiry of an access token properly is the key to a good UX and to stable authorization in an SPA.",null],[2150,"On receiving a `401 Unauthorized`, the frontend should determine whether the error has to do with an expired access token. Normally the token-refresh process through the refresh token is started. If the refresh succeeds, the original request is repeated. If it does not, the user is logged out. This approach lets you keep the user experience continuous.","A `401 Unauthorized` response is the main signal to the frontend of problems with authorization.\\n\\nThe basic scenario for handling a 401\\nOn receiving a `401` the frontend normally performs the following steps:\\n- It checks whether a token refresh is already under way\\n- It sends a request to refresh the access token\\n- It receives a new access token\\n- It repeats the original request\\n\\nThis process should be entirely automatic and unnoticeable to the user.\\n\\nProtection against the problem scenarios\\nThe frontend has to take into account:\\n- parallel requests with an expired token\\n- repeated `401`s after a refresh\\n- the risk of an infinite refresh loop\\n\\nFor that it normally:\\n- uses an `isRefreshing` flag\\n- has a queue of requests waiting for the refresh to finish\\n- introduces a limit on the number of attempts\\n\\nIf the refresh token is invalid\\nIn that case:\\n- the access token cannot be refreshed\\n- the session counts as finished\\n- the authorization state is cleared\\n- the user is redirected to the login page\\n\\nThe UX aspect\\nWhat matters:\\n- do not show the user API errors\\n- do not interrupt the current actions\\n- keep the form\'s data if you can\\n\\nConclusion: handling a `401 Unauthorized` properly makes authorization resilient and gives a smooth user experience.",null],[2151,"Adding an access token is normally implemented centrally, through a wrapper around the HTTP client. With Axios, request interceptors are used. For Fetch a custom wrapper function is created. That lets you avoid duplicated code and mistakes.","In frontend applications it is important to add the access token in the same way and automatically to every request that needs it.\\n\\nThe approach with Axios\\nIn Axios interceptors are the usual choice.\\n\\nThe principle:\\n- Before the request is sent, the interceptor intercepts the configuration\\n- It adds the `Authorization` header\\n- It returns the updated request\\n\\nThe approach with Fetch\\nFetch has no interceptors, so a wrapper is used.\\n\\nAn example of the logic:\\n- create an `apiFetch` function\\n- add the headers inside it\\n- call it instead of `fetch`\\n\\nThe advantages of the centralized approach\\n\\n- A single source of truth\\n- The logic is easier to update\\n- Fewer mistakes as things scale\\n\\nConclusion: adding the access token centrally through interceptors or a wrapper is the standard, safe solution for frontend applications.","axios.interceptors.request.use((config) => {\\n  config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${accessToken}`\\n  return config\\n})\\n\\nfunction apiFetch(url, options = {}) {\\n  return fetch(url, {\\n    ...options,\\n    headers: {\\n      ...options.headers,\\n      Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,\\n    },\\n  })\\n}"],[2152,"Axios interceptors let you intercept HTTP requests and responses before they are handled. With them you can change requests, add headers or handle errors. Interceptors work centrally and apply to every request. This makes maintenance easier and reduces code duplication. They are often used for authorization and for logging.","Interceptors in Axios are a mechanism that lets you build shared logic into the process of working with HTTP.\\n\\nWhat can be intercepted\\nAxios supports:\\n- request interceptors\\n- response interceptors\\n\\nThe typical tasks of request interceptors\\n\\n- Adding the access token\\n- Setting shared headers\\n- Logging the requests\\n- Substituting the configuration\\n\\nThe typical tasks of response interceptors\\n\\n- Handling errors (`401`, `403`)\\n- Starting the refresh-token flow\\n- Logging the responses\\n- Unifying the errors\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nInterceptors let you:\\n- avoid duplicating code\\n- centralize the network logic\\n- make the project easier to scale\\n\\nConclusion: Axios interceptors are a key tool for implementing shared network logic in an SPA.","axios.interceptors.response.use(\\n  response => response,\\n  error => {\\n    if (error.response?.status === 401) {\\n      // the token-refresh logic\\n    }\\n    return Promise.reject(error)\\n  }\\n)"],[2153,"Interceptors are convenient for tasks that have to be performed for every HTTP request. Those are adding the access token, handling errors and logging. They let you move repeating logic into a single place. This approach makes the code cleaner and more resilient.","Interceptors are especially useful for tasks that do not belong to the business logic but are needed by almost every request.\\n\\nThe main tasks for interceptors\\n\\n- Authorization\\n- adding the access token\\n- refreshing the tokens\\n\\n- Handling errors\\n- catching `401`, `403`, `500`\\n- unifying the error messages\\n\\n- Logging\\n- of requests\\n- of responses\\n- of the execution time\\n\\n- Modifying requests\\n- adding headers\\n- adding query parameters\\n\\nWhy this is convenient\\n\\n- The logic is held in a single place\\n- There is less duplicated code\\n- It is easier to maintain and to test\\n\\nWhat you should not do in interceptors\\n\\n- Hold business logic\\n- Work with the UI directly\\n- Change components\' state\\n\\nConclusion: interceptors are best suited to infrastructural tasks related to network interaction rather than to business logic.",null],[2154,"An SPA is an application that is loaded into the browser once and then works without the page reloading. When the user navigates, only the page\'s content changes rather than the whole document. The interaction with the server happens through an API. This approach makes the interface faster and more responsive. SPAs are widely used in React applications.","An SPA (Single Page Application) is a kind of web application where the browser loads one HTML page and all of the subsequent interaction happens on the client side.\\n\\nHow an SPA works\\nThe general principle looks like this:\\n- The browser loads the HTML, the CSS and the JavaScript\\n- The JavaScript takes control of the interface\\n- The navigation is handled on the client\\n- The data is loaded through an API without the page reloading\\n\\nThe role of the frontend frameworks\\nIn an SPA:\\n- React manages the state and the UI\\n- The routing is implemented on the client\\n- The backend most often provides a REST or GraphQL API\\n\\nThe advantages of an SPA\\n\\n- A fast-responding interface\\n- No page reloads\\n- A smoother user experience\\n\\nThe limitations of an SPA\\n\\n- Problems with SEO without additional solutions\\n- A large initial bundle\\n- A more complex architecture\\n\\nConclusion: an SPA is suited to interactive applications with a lot of logic on the client and frequent interaction with an API.",null],[2155,"An SPA is rendered entirely on the client side, whereas SSR is rendered on the server. In an SPA the user first downloads the JavaScript and then sees the content. With SSR the HTML is formed on the server and sent to the browser immediately. SSR improves SEO and the time to the first display. An SPA is simpler to implement but worse for search engines.","The main difference between an SPA and SSR lies in where the interface is rendered.\\n\\nSPA (Client-Side Rendering)\\nWith an SPA:\\n- the server serves an empty HTML template\\n- the whole UI is built in the browser\\n- the data is loaded once the application has initialized\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- a simple architecture\\n- high interactivity\\n- a lighter load on the server\\n\\nThe cons:\\n- a slow first render\\n- difficulties with SEO\\n- a dependence on JavaScript\\n\\nSSR (Server-Side Rendering)\\nWith SSR:\\n- the server generates the HTML with the data\\n- the browser displays the content immediately\\n- the JavaScript is attached later, for interactivity\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- a fast first render\\n- better indexing\\n- a more stable UX\\n\\nThe cons:\\n- a more complex architecture\\n- a heavier load on the server\\n- caching is harder\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\n\\n- SPA — admin panels, dashboards, internal services\\n- SSR — public sites, marketing, SEO-critical pages\\n\\nConclusion: SPAs and SSR solve different problems, and the choice of approach depends on the requirements for SEO, for performance and on the project\'s complexity.",null],[2156,"In frontend development unit, integration and e2e tests are used. Unit tests check individual functions or components. Integration tests check the interaction between the parts of the system. E2e tests imitate the user\'s behaviour. Each kind of test solves its own problem.","Frontend applications are tested at several levels, each of which solves a different problem.\\n\\nUnit tests\\nUnit tests check:\\n- individual functions\\n- hooks\\n- simple components\\n\\nTheir characteristics:\\n- fast\\n- isolated\\n- easy to maintain\\n\\nIntegration tests\\nIntegration tests check:\\n- the interaction between components\\n- the work with the API\\n- the correctness of the business scenarios\\n\\nTheir characteristics:\\n- closer to real use\\n- they reveal integration problems\\n\\nE2e tests\\nE2e tests check:\\n- the user scenarios\\n- the navigation\\n- forms and authorization\\n\\nTheir characteristics:\\n- as close to reality as possible\\n- slow\\n- hard to maintain\\n\\nThe balance of tests\\nNormally:\\n- more unit tests\\n- fewer integration ones\\n- a minimum of e2e\\n\\nConclusion: effective frontend testing is built on a combination of different kinds of test, each of which covers its own level of risk.",null],[2157,"Unit tests in frontend normally cover individual functions, hooks and simple components. They check isolated logic without any dependence on external services. Such tests run quickly and are easy to maintain. Their aim is to make sure the basic logic works correctly. Unit tests should not check complex user scenarios.","Unit tests in frontend are intended for checking the smallest, most isolated parts of the code.\\n\\nWhat is most often covered by unit tests\\n\\n- Utility functions\\n- formatting data\\n- validation\\n- transforming values\\n\\n- React hooks\\n- custom hooks\\n- state management\\n- side effects (with mocks)\\n\\n- Simple components\\n- rendering from props\\n- conditional output\\n- basic events\\n\\nWhat is normally not covered by unit tests\\n\\n- complex user scenarios\\n- interaction with a real API\\n- navigation between pages\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nUnit tests:\\n- find regressions quickly\\n- make refactoring easier\\n- document the code\'s behaviour\\n\\nConclusion: unit tests are effective for checking logic and simple components, but they are not suited to testing whole user behaviours.",null],[2158,"A `.env` file is used to hold environment variables. It normally holds the settings that depend on the environment. Those values are substituted into the application at build time. `.env` lets you avoid hard-coding the configuration in your code. This makes maintenance and deployment easier.","A `.env` file is intended for holding the configuration parameters that can differ between environments.\\n\\nWhat is normally held in `.env`\\n\\n- The API URL\\n- feature flags\\n- the keys of third-party services\\n- the application\'s operating modes\\n\\nHow it is used in frontend\\nIn frontend:\\n- the variables are read at build time\\n- the values are substituted into the code\\n- they often require a special prefix\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nWhy `.env` is convenient\\n\\n- there is no rigid tie to an environment\\n- it makes deployment easier\\n- it reduces the risk of configuration mistakes\\n\\nAn important limitation\\nEvery `.env` value that ends up in the frontend bundle:\\n- is visible to the user\\n- is not a secret\\n\\nConclusion: `.env` is convenient for configuration, but it is not suited to holding sensitive data in frontend.","const apiUrl = process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL"],[2159,"Sensitive data cannot be held in the code, because frontend code is available to the user. Anybody can look at the compiled JavaScript. That creates a risk of keys and tokens leaking. Even variables from `.env` are not protected. Secrets must be held only on the server.","A frontend application is by its nature publicly available, which makes holding secrets in it unsafe.\\n\\nWhy this is a problem\\nAny user can:\\n- open DevTools\\n- look at the network requests\\n- study the compiled JavaScript\\n\\nWhat data counts as sensitive\\n\\n- secret API keys\\n- private tokens\\n- client secrets\\n- passwords\\n\\nWhy `.env` does not solve the problem\\nIn frontend:\\n- `.env` is substituted at build time\\n- the values become part of the bundle\\n- the user can see them\\n\\nHow to work with secrets properly\\n\\n- hold the secrets on the backend\\n- proxy the requests through the server\\n- give the frontend only temporary tokens\\n\\nConclusion: you cannot hold secrets in frontend, because the code and the configuration are entirely available to the end user.",null],[2160,"Bugs in production are first recorded and assessed by severity. Critical problems are fixed through a hotfix. Less critical ones are planned into the backlog. The frontend analyses the logs and reproduces the problem locally. Once it has been fixed, a check and a deployment follow.","Bugs in production call for a fast, structured response, especially in frontend applications with a large number of users.\\n\\nThe typical process for handling production bugs\\n\\n- Discovery\\n- monitoring\\n- users\' errors\\n- alerts and logging\\n\\n- Assessing the severity\\n- does the bug block users\\n- does it affect money or data\\n- is there a workaround\\n\\n- The response\\n- critical → a hotfix\\n- not critical → the backlog\\n\\nThe frontend\'s role in the investigation\\nThe frontend normally:\\n- analyses the stack trace\\n- looks at the Network tab and the logs\\n- checks the latest changes\\n- reproduces the bug locally\\n\\nAfter the fix\\nWhat matters:\\n- check the scenario again\\n- make sure there are no regressions\\n- document the cause\\n- add a test if necessary\\n\\nBad practices\\n\\n- fixing things without analysing the cause\\n- deploying without checking\\n- ignoring recurring bugs\\n\\nConclusion: working with bugs in production is not only about a fast fix but about analysing the causes, so as to prevent the problem recurring.",null],[2161,"A re-render happens when React considers that a component\'s data has changed.\\nMost often that has to do with a change of `state` or `props`.\\nA re-render can also happen because of a change of context.\\nUnderstanding these causes matters for optimizing performance.","A re-render is a repeated call of a component\'s function or of its `render` method.\\n\\nThe main causes of a re-render\\nReact starts a re-render if:\\n- the component\'s `state` has changed\\n- new `props` have arrived\\n- a `Context` value has changed\\n- the parent component has re-rendered\\n\\nA change of `state`\\nAny call to `setState` or `useState`:\\n- marks the component for an update\\n- causes a repeated render\\n\\nA change of `props`\\nIf the parent passes new values:\\n- React compares the references\\n- if they differ, a re-render starts\\n\\nContext\\nWhen a `Context` value changes:\\n- every subscribed component re-renders\\n- even if they use only part of the data\\n\\nWhat is important to remember\\nA re-render:\\n- does not mean the DOM is updated\\n- can be interrupted by optimizations\\n- is a normal process\\n\\nIn short\\nA React component re-renders when its `state`, `props` or `context` change, or because of its parent. That is the basis of React\'s reactive model.","setCount(count + 1);"],[2162,"Unnecessary re-renders can be prevented by means of memoization.\\nMost often `React.memo`, `useMemo` and `useCallback` are used.\\nIt is also important to keep an eye on which `props` are passed down to the child components.\\nOptimization reduces the load on rendering and improves performance.","By default React re-renders the child components on every render of the parent.\\n\\nWhy this happens\\nReact considers that:\\n- the `props` may have changed during the parent\'s render\\n- components are pure functions\\n- calling them again is safe\\n\\nUsing `React.memo`\\n`React.memo` lets you:\\n- remember the result of a component\'s render\\n- skip the re-render if the `props` have not changed\\n\\nMemoizing values and functions\\nIf the parent passes:\\n- objects\\n- arrays\\n- functions\\n\\nthey need to be memoized:\\n\\nSplitting components up\\nWhat also helps:\\n- moving logic into separate components\\n- reducing the area that updates\\n\\nWhen optimization is not needed\\nIt is not worth optimizing:\\n- simple components\\n- code with no performance problems\\n\\nIn short\\nUnnecessary re-renders are prevented by memoization and by passing `props` carefully. Optimization should be applied deliberately.","const Child = React.memo(function Child({ value }) {\\n  return <span>{value}</span>;\\n});\\n\\nconst data = useMemo(() => ({ a: 1 }), []);\\nconst handler = useCallback(() => {}, []);"],[2163,"Code on unmounting is run through `useEffect`.\\nFor that, a clean-up function is returned from the effect.\\nThat function is called before the component is removed from the DOM.\\nThis approach is used to clean resources up.","Unmounting is the moment when a component is removed from the interface.\\n\\nA definition\\nUnmounting is the phase of the lifecycle in which React removes a component and frees the resources associated with it.\\n\\nUsing `useEffect`\\nThe clean-up function is specified like this:\\n\\nWhen the clean-up is called\\nThe clean-up function is run:\\n- when the component unmounts\\n- before the effect runs again\\n\\nThe typical scenarios\\nMost often what is cleaned up is:\\n- timers\\n- subscriptions\\n- event handlers\\n- WebSocket connections\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nWithout clean-up:\\n- memory leaks arise\\n- the code goes on running after the component has been removed\\n\\nIn short\\nTo run code on unmounting, the clean-up function in `useEffect` is used. It is an essential practice when working with effects.","useEffect(() => {\\n  // the initialization\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    // the clean-up on unmounting\\n  };\\n}, []);"],[2164,"`React.memo` is a mechanism for memoizing functional components.\\nIt prevents a re-render if the component\'s `props` have not changed.\\nThe comparison is done by reference.\\nThis helps cut down the amount of unnecessary computation during rendering.","By default React re-renders a component every time its parent re-renders.\\n\\nA definition\\n`React.memo` is an HOC that remembers the result of a component\'s render and reuses it if the incoming `props` have not changed.\\n\\nHow the comparison works\\nReact:\\n- compares the previous and the new `props`\\n- uses a shallow comparison\\n- checks the references rather than the contents of objects\\n\\nA custom comparison function\\nYou can pass a comparison function of your own:\\n\\nWhen `React.memo` does not help\\nThe optimization will not work if:\\n- new objects or functions are passed in the `props`\\n- the parent creates the values on every render\\n\\nWhen to use it\\n`React.memo` is useful if:\\n- the component re-renders often\\n- the render is expensive\\n- the `props` rarely change\\n\\nIn short\\n`React.memo` reduces the number of re-renders by means of memoization, but it is effective only when the `props` are stable.","const User = React.memo(function User({ name }) {\\n  return <span>{name}</span>;\\n});\\n\\nReact.memo(Component, (prevProps, nextProps) => {\\n  return prevProps.id === nextProps.id;\\n});"],[2165,"`useRef` returns an object with a `current` field.\\nThe value of `current` is preserved between renders.\\nChanging `current` does not cause a re-render.\\nMost often `useRef` is used to access the DOM or to hold auxiliary data.","`useRef` is a way of holding data that survives a render but does not affect the UI.\\n\\nA definition\\n`useRef` is a hook that returns a stable object of the form `{ current: value }`.\\n\\nThe behaviour of `current`\\nThe characteristics:\\n- the value is preserved between renders\\n- changing `current` does not cause the component to update\\n- the reference to the object stays the same\\n\\nAccess to the DOM\\nThe frequent scenario is working with DOM elements:\\n\\nHolding auxiliary data\\n`useRef` is used for:\\n- timers\\n- previous values\\n- flags and counters\\n\\nHow `useRef` differs from `useState`\\nUnlike `useState`:\\n- `useRef` does not cause a re-render\\n- it is suited to data that does not affect the display\\n\\nIn short\\n`useRef` holds mutable data between renders without affecting the UI. It is a convenient tool for working with the DOM and with auxiliary state.","const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n<input ref={inputRef} />\\n// inputRef.current points to the DOM element"],[2166,"Changing `ref.current` does not cause the component to re-render.\\nReact does not track changes inside a `ref`.\\nThat is deliberate, so that auxiliary data can be held there.\\n`useRef` is therefore suited to values that should not affect the UI.","It is important to be clear about which changes React tracks and which it does not.\\n\\nA definition\\n`ref.current` is a mutable field of the object returned by `useRef`, changes to which take no part in React\'s rendering mechanism.\\n\\nWhy no re-render happens\\nReact:\\n- tracks changes to `state` and `props`\\n- does not subscribe to changes to `ref.current`\\n- treats a `ref` as a store outside the UI logic\\n\\nWhen this is useful\\nThis behaviour is convenient for:\\n- holding timers\\n- holding previous values\\n- counters that do not affect the interface\\n- integrations with external APIs\\n\\nA common mistake\\nBeginners often:\\n- try to hold UI data in a `ref`\\n- expect the interface to update automatically\\n\\nIf the data affects the display, `useState` has to be used.\\n\\nIn short\\nChanging `ref.current` does not cause a re-render. `useRef` is intended for data unrelated to the UI.","const countRef = useRef(0);\\n\\ncountRef.current += 1;\\n// the component will not re-render"],[2167,"Context should not be used for frequently changing data.\\nIt can cause mass re-renders of components.\\nContext is also awkward for complex business logic.\\nIn such cases it is better to use specialized state managers.","React Context is a powerful tool, but it is not suited to every task.\\n\\nA definition\\nContext is a mechanism for passing data through the component tree without passing `props` explicitly.\\n\\nWhen Context becomes a problem\\nContext is not recommended if:\\n- the value updates often\\n- there is a large number of subscribers\\n- the data is used selectively\\n- complex update logic is required\\n\\nThe problem of re-renders\\nWhen a Context value changes:\\n- every consumer re-renders\\n- even if only part of the data is used\\n\\nThat can have a negative effect on performance.\\n\\nThe architectural limitations\\nContext is poorly suited to:\\n- complex dependencies between data\\n- large-scale applications\\n- predictable state management\\n\\nWhat Context is replaced with\\nIn such cases people use:\\n- Redux\\n- Zustand\\n- MobX\\n\\nThese solutions give more precise control over updates.\\n\\nIn short\\nContext is convenient for rare, global data. For frequently changing state it becomes a bottleneck.",null],[2168,"Controlled components are managed through `state`.\\nUncontrolled components hold their state inside the DOM.\\nControlled ones give more control and predictability.\\nUncontrolled ones are simpler but less flexible.","Working with forms is one of the basic scenarios in React.\\n\\nA definition\\n\\n- A controlled component — the element\'s value is managed through `state`\\n- An uncontrolled component — the value is held in the DOM and read through a `ref`\\n\\nControlled components\\nThe characteristics:\\n- the value is set through `value`\\n- the changes are handled through `onChange`\\n- the state is always in sync with the UI\\n\\nUncontrolled components\\nThe characteristics:\\n- the value is held in the DOM\\n- `defaultValue` is used\\n- the reading happens through a `ref`\\n\\nA comparison of the approaches\\nControlled:\\n- easier to validate\\n- more convenient to test\\n- easier to keep in sync\\n\\nUncontrolled:\\n- less code\\n- suited to simple forms\\n\\nIn short\\nControlled components are preferable for complex forms and business logic. Uncontrolled ones are suited to simple scenarios.","const [value, setValue] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n<input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />\\n\\n<input ref={inputRef} defaultValue=\\"test\\" />"],[2169,"A group of checkboxes with a single piece of state is implemented through shared `state`.\\nEach checkbox reflects part of that state.\\nWhen one checkbox changes, the shared state is updated.\\nThe UI is always rendered from the current data.","When working with several checkboxes it is important that their state be centralized and predictable.\\n\\nA definition\\nA single piece of state for a group of checkboxes is a shared object or array describing which items are selected.\\n\\nThe approach with an array of values\\nAn array of the selected values is the usual choice:\\n\\nUpdating the state:\\n- on selection — we add the value\\n- on deselection — we remove the value\\n\\nThe link between the state and the UI\\nEach checkbox:\\n- reads its own state from the `state`\\n- reports changes through a handler\\n\\nWhy this is correct\\nThis approach:\\n- rules out the UI and the data getting out of sync\\n- makes validation easier\\n- makes submitting the form easier\\n\\nIn short\\nA group of checkboxes should be managed by a single piece of state. That makes the form\'s behaviour predictable and manageable.","const [selected, setSelected] = useState([]);\\n\\nconst toggle = value => {\\n  setSelected(prev =>\\n    prev.includes(value)\\n      ? prev.filter(v => v !== value)\\n      : [...prev, value]\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n<input\\n  type=\\"checkbox\\"\\n  checked={selected.includes(\'a\')}\\n  onChange={() => toggle(\'a\')}\\n/>"],[2170,"React Context lets you hold the state of a group of checkboxes in a single place.\\nThe components access the data directly through the Context.\\nThat saves you from prop drilling.\\nThe approach is useful for complex forms and nested structures.","When checkboxes sit deep in the component tree, passing the state through `props` becomes awkward.\\n\\nA definition\\nReact Context is a mechanism for global access to data within part of the component tree.\\n\\nHolding the state in a Context\\nWhat is normally put in the Context:\\n- the checkboxes\' current state\\n- the functions for changing it\\n\\nUsing it in the checkboxes\\nInside any child component:\\n\\nThat lets you:\\n- read the shared state\\n- change it without the intermediate components\\n\\nWhen this is justified\\nContext is suitable if:\\n- the checkboxes are spread across different components\\n- the form is complex and nested\\n- it matters to avoid drilling `props` unnecessarily\\n\\nThe limitations\\nIt has to be remembered that:\\n- a change to the Context causes every consumer to re-render\\n- for frequently changing data that can be expensive\\n\\nIn short\\nContext is convenient for managing a group of checkboxes in a complex hierarchy, but it calls for care because of the re-renders.","const CheckboxContext = React.createContext(null);\\n\\n<CheckboxContext.Provider value={{ selected, toggle }}>\\n  {children}\\n</CheckboxContext.Provider>\\n\\nconst { selected, toggle } = useContext(CheckboxContext);"],[2171,"A re-render is a repeated run of a component\'s logic.\\nFrequent re-renders increase the load on the CPU and on the browser\'s main thread.\\nThat can lead to lag and a \\"jerky\\" interface.\\nOptimizing re-renders makes the UI more responsive and more stable.","React builds an interface through frequent re-renders, but their number and their cost matter.\\n\\nA definition\\nA re-render is a repeated call of a component\'s function, followed by a comparison of the Virtual DOM and a possible update to the real DOM.\\n\\nWhy re-renders are expensive\\nEvery re-render:\\n- runs the component\'s code\\n- recomputes the JSX\\n- may run hooks\\n- increases the load on the Event Loop\\n\\nEven if the DOM is not updated, the computation happens all the same.\\n\\nThe effect on the UI\\nExcessive re-renders lead to:\\n- dropped frames\\n- input delays\\n- problems with animations\\n- a feeling that the interface is \\"sluggish\\"\\n\\nWhere this is especially critical\\nOptimization matters:\\n- in large component trees\\n- with complex computations in the render\\n- with frequent state updates\\n- in lists and tables\\n\\nThe main optimization tools\\nThe usual choices are:\\n- `React.memo`\\n- `useMemo`\\n- `useCallback`\\n- a proper decomposition of the components\\n- moving heavy logic out of the render\\n\\nIn short\\nOptimizing re-renders in React matters to performance and to the UX. The fewer unnecessary renders there are, the more smoothly and stably the application runs.",null],[2172,"The Virtual DOM is a lightweight representation of the real DOM in memory. React first updates the Virtual DOM and then compares it against the previous version. From that comparison the minimal set of changes for the real DOM is computed. That lets it avoid unnecessary DOM operations. As a result the interface updates faster and more efficiently.","Working with the real DOM is an expensive operation, so React uses an intermediate layer in the form of the Virtual DOM.\\n\\nA definition\\nThe Virtual DOM is an object representation of the DOM tree, held in memory and describing the structure of the interface.\\n\\nThe general update process\\nWhen a component\'s state or props change, React performs several steps:\\n- It creates a new Virtual DOM tree\\n- It compares it against the previous version\\n- It finds the differences\\n- It applies the minimal changes to the real DOM\\n\\nThis process is often called reconciliation.\\n\\nWhy this is efficient\\nIt is important to understand that React:\\n- does not update the DOM directly on every change\\n- groups the changes\\n- minimizes the number of operations on the real DOM\\n\\nRoughly it looks like this:\\n\\nThe browser\'s role\\nReact does not replace the browser:\\n- the browser is still responsible for painting\\n- React merely prepares the optimal set of changes\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Virtual DOM lets React manage updates to the interface efficiently, reducing the number of direct operations on the real DOM and improving the application\'s performance.","setState({ count: count + 1 });\\n// React decides for itself which DOM nodes need updating"],[2173,"React compares Virtual DOM elements by the element\'s type and by its key. If the element\'s type differs, React recreates the subtree entirely. If the type matches, React updates only the attributes that have changed. For lists the `key` is taken into account as well. This approach makes the comparison fast but not perfect.","React uses a heuristic comparison algorithm rather than a full diff of every node.\\n\\nThe main rules of the comparison\\nReact relies on several key criteria:\\n- The element\'s type\\n- The key (`key`)\\n- The props\\n- The position in the tree\\n\\nThe element\'s type\\nIf the element\'s type has changed:\\n\\nReact:\\n- removes the old element\\n- creates a new one\\n- recreates the whole subtree\\n\\nThe same element type\\nIf the type matches:\\n- React compares the props\\n- it updates only the values that have changed\\n- it preserves the component\'s state\\n\\nWorking with lists\\nIn lists React:\\n- compares the elements by `key`\\n- does not go by the array index\\n- tries to keep the correspondence between the elements\\n\\nThe algorithm\'s limitations\\nReact:\\n- does not analyse a subtree\'s content deeply\\n- assumes that identical types behave in a similar way\\n- sacrifices accuracy for speed\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact compares Virtual DOM elements by type and by `key`, using a simplified, fast algorithm. That lets it update the interface efficiently, but it calls for careful use of lists and keys.","<div /> → <span />"],[2174,"A `key` lets React identify the elements of a list unambiguously. Thanks to that, React understands which elements have been added, removed or moved. Without a `key` React goes by the index, which can lead to bugs. Correct keys help preserve the components\' state. This matters both to performance and to the interface being correct.","When working with lists React faces the task of matching the elements up between renders.\\n\\nWhat a `key` is\\nA key is an element\'s unique identifier within a list, which helps React track it across updates.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhat happens without a `key`\\nIf there is no `key`:\\n- React uses the array index\\n- when the order changes the elements get confused\\n- a component\'s state can \\"move\\" to a different element\\n\\nThe typical problems\\n\\n- data updated incorrectly\\n- loss of focus in inputs\\n- incorrect animations\\n- bugs that are hard to reproduce\\n\\nWhat a `key` should be\\n\\n- stable\\n- unique\\n- independent of the position in the array\\n\\nUsing the index is acceptable only for static lists that do not change.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe `key` is a critically important mechanism for lists working correctly in React. It lets you match elements up precisely and avoid bugs when the interface updates.","items.map(item => (\\n  <ListItem key={item.id} value={item} />\\n));"],[2175,"Yes, changing the `key` leads to the component being remounted entirely. React treats an element with a new `key` as a completely new one. As a result the old component unmounts and a new one is created from scratch. That leads to the state and the effects being reset. The technique is used deliberately and selectively.","React uses the `key` as part of an element\'s identity in the tree. If the identity changes, React does not update the component but recreates it.\\n\\nHow React treats a `key`\\nFor React an element is determined not only by its type but by the value of its `key`:\\n- the same type + the same `key` → an update\\n- the same type + a different `key` → a remount\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhen `userId` changes:\\n- the old component unmounts\\n- the state (`useState`) is reset\\n- the effects are cleaned up\\n- the component mounts again\\n\\nWhen this is useful\\nUsing a `key` for a remount is justified if you need to:\\n- reset the internal state entirely\\n- recreate a third-party library\\n- restart animations come what may\\n- avoid complicated state-synchronization logic\\n\\nWhen it is a bad idea\\nYou should not use a `key`:\\n- as a way of \\"fixing\\" bugs\\n- for frequent updates\\n- instead of managing the state properly\\n\\nThat can lead to:\\n- unnecessary renders\\n- the loss of the user\'s data\\n- worse performance\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `key` can be used to force a remount, but it is a tool for rare, deliberate cases. In most situations it is better to manage the state directly.","<Component key={userId} />"],[2176,"If you do not pass an array of dependencies, the effect will run after every render. That includes both the first render and every subsequent update. This behaviour often leads to unnecessary operations. In some cases it can cause an infinite loop. An effect without dependencies has to be used very carefully.","The absence of an array of dependencies is a deliberate but dangerous mode of operation for `useEffect`.\\n\\nHow React behaves in that case\\nIf no array is given:\\n\\nReact:\\n- runs the effect after every render\\n- performs no optimization at all\\n- assumes the effect depends on everything\\n\\nThe potential problems\\nSuch an effect can:\\n- perform network requests on every render\\n- call `setState` and start a new render\\n- create infinite loops\\n\\nAn example of the problem code:\\n\\nWhen it is acceptable\\nThe absence of the array can be justified if:\\n- the effect has to synchronize with every render\\n- operations with no side updates are performed\\n- the code is guaranteed to be safe\\n\\nSuch cases are rare.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useEffect` without an array of dependencies runs after every render. In most cases that leads to problems, so the dependencies should be stated explicitly.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"effect\\");\\n});\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  setCount(count + 1);\\n});"],[2177,"`useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously after the DOM has changed but before the browser paints. Until the code inside the effect has finished, the browser cannot show the updated interface. If the effect contains heavy computations, the render visually \\"hangs\\". That is precisely why using `useLayoutEffect` incorrectly can make performance worse. This hook calls for particular care.","To understand the cause of the blocking, it is important to be clear about the order in which React\'s render stages run.\\n\\nThe order of execution\\nAfter the state updates, React performs the following steps:\\n- It computes the new Virtual DOM\\n- It applies the changes to the real DOM\\n- It runs `useLayoutEffect`\\n- It hands control to the browser for painting\\n- It runs `useEffect`\\n\\nThe key point is that `useLayoutEffect` runs before the paint.\\n\\nWhy the blocking arises\\nWhile the code in `useLayoutEffect` is running:\\n- the browser cannot paint the screen\\n- the user sees a delay\\n- the interface seems \\"frozen\\"\\n\\nAn example of the potential problem:\\n\\nIf inside it there are:\\n- complex computations\\n- synchronous loops\\n- a great deal of work with layout metrics\\n\\nthe blocking becomes noticeable.\\n\\nThe difference from `useEffect`\\n`useEffect`:\\n- runs after the paint\\n- does not block the UI\\n- is suited to most side effects\\n\\n`useLayoutEffect`:\\n- is synchronous\\n- blocks the paint\\n- is needed only in special cases\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useLayoutEffect` can block rendering, because it runs before the browser paints. It is worth using only when the effect really does have to run before the UI is shown.","useLayoutEffect(() => {\\n  // heavy computations or synchronous measurements\\n});"],[2178,"`useLayoutEffect` is worth using when you need to read or change the DOM synchronously before the paint. That is relevant for measuring the sizes of elements and for preventing visual jumps. It is also used for integrating with libraries that work with the DOM directly. In most cases `useEffect` is enough. `useLayoutEffect` is a tool for specific tasks.","`useLayoutEffect` exists not as an alternative to `useEffect` but as a specialized tool.\\n\\nThe typical usage scenarios\\n`useLayoutEffect` is justified when you need to:\\n- measure the sizes of DOM elements (`width`, `height`)\\n- compute an element\'s position before the paint\\n- correct the layout synchronously\\n- prevent visual \\"flicker\\"\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWorking with external libraries\\nSome libraries:\\n- change the DOM directly\\n- expect synchronous execution\\n\\nIn such cases `useLayoutEffect` lets you integrate them into React properly.\\n\\nWhen not to use it\\nYou should not apply `useLayoutEffect` if:\\n- the effect does not depend on sizes or on the layout\\n- you can wait for the paint\\n- there are no visual artefacts\\n\\nIn all of those cases it is better to choose `useEffect`.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useLayoutEffect` is needed for working with the DOM synchronously before the paint. It is a powerful but dangerous tool, which is worth applying only when it is really necessary.","useLayoutEffect(() => {\\n  const rect = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();\\n  setHeight(rect.height);\\n}, []);"],[2179,"`setState` in React is not strictly synchronous or asynchronous. React can combine several state updates as an optimization. The state update does not happen immediately but is scheduled. Immediately after a `setState` call, therefore, the new value may not be available. This behaviour depends on the execution mode and on the environment.","`setState` is often called asynchronous, but that simplification is not quite accurate.\\n\\nHow React handles `setState`\\nWhen `setState` or `setCount` is called:\\n- React schedules the update\\n- several updates may be combined\\n- the actual render happens later\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThis happens because React has not applied the update yet.\\n\\nBatching updates\\nReact groups updates:\\n- inside event handlers\\n- inside effects\\n- in asynchronous code (in the modern versions)\\n\\nThat reduces the number of renders and improves performance.\\n\\nThe functional form of `setState`\\nTo work with the current state, the functional form is used:\\n\\nReact guarantees that `prev` is the latest value.\\n\\nThe important conclusion\\n`setState`:\\n- does not block the execution of the code\\n- does not guarantee an immediate update\\n- obeys React\'s optimization strategy\\n\\nConclusion\\n`setState` cannot strictly be called either synchronous or asynchronous. It is a scheduled update, which React performs at a moment convenient to itself.","setCount(count + 1);\\nconsole.log(count); // the old value\\n\\nsetCount(prev => prev + 1);"],[2180,"`React.memo` compares the props of the current and the previous render. By default a shallow comparison is used. If every prop counts as the same, the component does not re-render. If at least one prop has changed, the render happens again. You can also pass a comparison function of your own.","`React.memo` is an optimization tool that lets you skip unnecessary renders of components.\\n\\nA definition\\nReact.memo is an HOC that memoizes the result of a functional component\'s render on the basis of its props.\\n\\nHow the comparison works\\nBy default React:\\n- compares the props by reference\\n- uses `Object.is`\\n- performs no deep comparison\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIf `value` is a primitive and has not changed, the render will be skipped.\\n\\nWhat counts as a change\\nA re-render will happen if:\\n- a primitive has changed (`number`, `string`, `boolean`)\\n- a new object has been passed\\n- a new function has been passed\\n- the `key` has changed\\n\\nA custom comparison function\\nYou can pass a comparator of your own:\\n\\nBut:\\n- it complicates the code\\n- it can be more expensive than the render itself\\n\\nThe limitations\\n`React.memo`:\\n- does not prevent the parent from rendering\\n- does not guarantee a speed-up\\n- is useful only when the render is expensive\\n\\nConclusion\\n`React.memo` decides about a re-render on the basis of a shallow comparison of the props. Its effectiveness depends on the stability of the data passed in.","const MyComponent = React.memo(function MyComponent({ value }) {\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nReact.memo(Component, (prevProps, nextProps) => {\\n  return prevProps.id === nextProps.id;\\n});"],[2181,"Functions in JavaScript are objects, and a new function is created on every render. To `React.memo` that looks like a change of prop. As a result the memoization does not take effect. Even if the function\'s logic has not changed, the reference to it is new. This is a frequent reason why `React.memo` is used to no purpose.","To understand the problem it is important to remember how functions are created in JavaScript.\\n\\nWhy the reference changes\\nOn every render:\\n\\nWhat is created is:\\n- a new function\\n- a new reference\\n- a new prop\\n\\nTo `React.memo` that means the prop has changed.\\n\\nThe consequences\\nBecause of this:\\n- the child component re-renders\\n- the memoization loses its point\\n- the optimization does not work\\n\\nEven if the function does the same thing, React cannot determine that.\\n\\nA frequent mistake\\nUsing `React.memo` without stabilizing the functions:\\n- creates a false sense of optimization\\n- complicates the code\\n- gives no gain in performance\\n\\nConclusion\\nPassing functions through props breaks memoization because the reference changes. That is normal JavaScript behaviour, not a React bug.","<Component onClick={() => doSomething()} />"],[2182,"To stop a function breaking memoization, it has to be memoized. For that `useCallback` is used. It returns one and the same reference between renders as long as the dependencies have not changed. That lets `React.memo` compare the props properly. `useCallback` should be used deliberately, however.","The solution to the problem of unstable functions is managing the references.\\n\\nUsing `useCallback`\\n`useCallback` memoizes a function:\\n\\nNow:\\n- the reference to the function is stable\\n- the function changes only when `id` changes\\n- `React.memo` can work properly\\n\\nHow to choose the dependencies\\nThe array of dependencies has to include:\\n- every variable from the outer scope\\n- the values used inside the function\\n\\nOmitting dependencies leads to:\\n- stale data\\n- logic errors\\n\\nWhen `useCallback` is not needed\\nYou should not use `useCallback` if:\\n- the component is not memoized\\n- the function is not passed down to child components\\n- no optimization is required\\n\\nThe cost of memoization\\n`useCallback`:\\n- holds the function in memory\\n- requires the dependencies to be compared\\n- can be more expensive than recreating the function\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo stop a function breaking memoization, its reference has to be stabilized by means of `useCallback`. This hook is useful only in combination with `React.memo` and expensive components.","const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n  doSomething(id);\\n}, [id]);"],[2183,"Modal windows are implemented through portals so that they can be rendered outside the ordinary DOM hierarchy. That helps you avoid problems with `z-index`, `overflow` and positioning. The modal nevertheless stays part of the React tree. Portals let you keep React\'s logic and make styling easier. It is the standard, recommended approach.","Visually a modal window has to sit above the whole interface, while logically it belongs to the current screen.\\n\\nA definition\\nA Portal is a React mechanism that lets you render a component into a different DOM node without disturbing the hierarchy of React components.\\n\\nThe main problem without portals\\nIf you render a modal inside the ordinary DOM tree:\\n- it can be clipped because of `overflow: hidden`\\n- `z-index` conflicts arise\\n- positioning becomes harder\\n- accessibility suffers (focus, scroll)\\n\\nHow portals solve the problem\\nA portal lets you:\\n- Render the modal in `document.body`, for example\\n- Avoid the influence of the parent\'s CSS constraints\\n- Keep the context, the props and the state\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nAn important point\\nAlthough the DOM node is different:\\n- events bubble correctly\\n- Context goes on working\\n- the application\'s logic does not break\\n\\nConclusion\\nPortals let you implement modal windows properly, separating the visual placement from the application\'s logical structure.","ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n  <Modal />,\\n  document.body\\n);"],[2184,"In React data is most often passed through props from the top down. For feedback in the other direction, callback functions are used. The Context API and global state managers are also used. For local state, hooks are used. The choice of method depends on the scale and the complexity of the application.","In React there are several main ways of passing data, each of which solves a different problem.\\n\\nPassing through props\\nThe most basic and the recommended way:\\n- the data is passed from the parent to the child\\n- the data flow is one-way\\n- the code is easy to follow\\n\\nPassing back through callbacks\\nTo pass data from the bottom up:\\n\\nThe parent controls the state while the child component reports events.\\n\\nThe Context API\\nIt is used for:\\n- passing data deep into the tree\\n- avoiding prop drilling\\n\\nIt is suited to:\\n- the theme\\n- localization\\n- authorization\\n\\nGlobal state managers\\nThey are used when:\\n- the state is used in many parts of the application\\n- complex logic is required\\n- scalability matters\\n\\nLocal state\\nFor isolated data people use:\\n- `useState`\\n- `useReducer`\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of how to pass data depends on the scope of the state. The closer the data sits to where it is used, the simpler and more reliable the architecture.","<Child value={value} />\\n\\n<Child onChange={handleChange} />"],[2185,"The Context API is poorly suited to frequently changing state. When the context updates, every subscriber re-renders. That can lead to performance problems. Context also provides no tools for complex logic. For large-scale applications, therefore, state managers are often used.","The Context API solves the problem of accessing data, but it is not a fully fledged state manager.\\n\\nThe Context API\'s main limitations\\nContext becomes a problem if:\\n- the state updates often\\n- the context contains a great deal of data\\n- there are many subscribers\\n- performance matters\\n\\nWhen the value changes:\\n- every consumer of the context re-renders\\n- it is hard to optimize the updates\\n\\nThe absence of infrastructure\\nThe Context API:\\n- gives no tools for side effects\\n- does not manage asynchronous logic\\n- provides no middleware\\n- has no devtools of the kind the state managers have\\n\\nWhen Context is suitable\\nContext is good for:\\n- rare changes\\n- global settings\\n- configuration data\\n\\nWhen a state manager is better\\nA state manager is justified if:\\n- the business scenarios are complex\\n- there is a lot of dependent state\\n- a strict architecture is needed\\n- control over the updates is required\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Context API is a mechanism for delivering data, not a fully fledged solution for managing state. As an application grows it often loses out to the state managers.",null],[2186,"For testing React, Jest and React Testing Library are the most common choices. Jest is responsible for running the tests and for mocks. React Testing Library is used to test components\' behaviour. For e2e tests, Cypress or Playwright are used. The choice of tools depends on the kind of testing.","The testing of React applications is normally built at several levels.\\n\\nUnit and component tests\\nThe most widespread combination:\\n- Jest — the test runner and the assertion library\\n- React Testing Library — testing components through the user\'s behaviour\\n\\nReact Testing Library\'s approach:\\n- test the result rather than the implementation\\n- find elements the way the user does\\n- minimize the dependence on the internal structure\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nMocks and isolation\\nJest lets you:\\n- mock functions\\n- substitute modules\\n- control timers\\n- isolate the logic under test\\n\\nEnd-to-end testing\\nFor e2e tests people use:\\n- Cypress\\n- Playwright\\n\\nThey check:\\n- the working of the whole application\\n- real user scenarios\\n- the integration of the frontend with the API\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact is normally tested by means of Jest and React Testing Library, with separate tools used for the e2e scenarios. This approach covers the application\'s behaviour at the various levels.","render(<Button />);\\nexpect(screen.getByText(\\"Save\\")).toBeInTheDocument();"],[2187,"Complex animations have to be optimized by reducing the weight of the assets, moving the computation to the GPU and cutting down the number of repaints. `transform` and `opacity` are the most common choices, since they cause neither layout nor repaint. It is also important to load animations lazily and to disable them on weak devices. In React applications it is critical to keep control of the number of re-renders. Optimization is a balance between visual quality and performance.","Complex animations can have a strong effect on the FPS, on the loading time and on the interface\'s responsiveness, so optimizing them is an essential part of frontend development.\\n\\nWhat exactly affects animation performance\\nBefore optimizing, it is important to understand the main sources of problems:\\n- the large size of the assets (video, SVG, JSON animations)\\n- frequent layout and repaint\\n- JavaScript animations performed on the main thread\\n- unnecessary re-renders of React components\\n\\nThe main approaches to optimization\\n\\n- The use of GPU-friendly properties\\nAnimations are worth building around:\\n- `transform`\\n- `opacity`\\nThese properties do not cause layout to be recomputed and work through compositing.\\n\\n- Reducing the weight of the animation assets\\n- reducing the resolution of videos\\n- optimizing SVGs (removing unnecessary paths and metadata)\\n- simplifying the keyframe animations\\n\\n- Keeping control of the re-renders in React\\n- moving the animations outside React\'s render\\n- using `memo`, if the animation is wrapped in a component\\n- separating the logic from the visual part\\n\\n- Lazy loading of the animations\\n- loading an animation only when it appears in the viewport\\n- loading heavy animations after `DOMContentLoaded`\\n\\n- Adapting to the user\'s device\\n- disabling complex animations on weak devices\\n- taking `prefers-reduced-motion` into account\\n\\nConclusion\\nComplex animations should be technically simple for the browser: a minimal weight, GPU properties and a minimum of JavaScript logic. The earlier optimization is built in, the fewer performance problems there will be in a real project.","@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\\n  .animation {\\n    animation: none;\\n  }\\n}"],[2188,"Multi-stage animations let you achieve a wow effect and give flexibility, but they complicate the implementation and the maintenance. They increase the number of assets and the amount of synchronization logic. Such animations are often harder to optimize and to test. They are used when a single format does not give the result you need. It is important to understand whether such complexity is justified.","Multi-stage animations are a chain of different technologies, each of which solves its own problem.\\n\\nWhy multi-stage animations are used\\nMost often it is done for:\\n- complex intro screens\\n- marketing pages\\n- brand animations\\n\\nFor example:\\n- video for a striking start\\n- SVG for interactive transitions\\n- `transform` for the final UI animations\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach\\n\\n- High visual quality\\n- video gives complex effects\\n- SVG lets you control the individual elements\\n\\n- Flexibility\\n- each stage can be optimized separately\\n- individual stages can be disabled\\n\\n- Control over the user\'s perception\\n- gradual engagement\\n- smooth transitions between states\\n\\nThe disadvantages and the risks\\n\\n- Growing complexity\\n- more code\\n- complicated synchronization of the timings\\n\\n- Performance problems\\n- more assets\\n- a heavier load on the CPU and the GPU\\n\\n- Difficulty of maintenance\\n- harder to test\\n- harder to change\\n\\nWhen the approach is justified\\n\\n- landing pages\\n- promotional projects\\n- situations where the visuals are the key value\\n\\nConclusion\\nMulti-stage animations give a powerful visual effect, but they call for strict control of the complexity and the performance. In ordinary interfaces simple, predictable animations more often win out.",null],[2189,"React Native and React use the same approach to building a UI, but they render it differently. In React the UI is displayed in the browser\'s DOM; in React Native it is displayed in the platform\'s native components. The logic and the hooks are similar, but the API and the styling differ. React Native is closer to mobile development than to the web. Code cannot simply be taken and moved across unchanged.","React Native is often perceived as \\"React for mobile\\", but that is a simplified picture.\\n\\nWhat they have in common\\nReact and React Native share the key ideas:\\n- the component-based approach\\n- hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`)\\n- a unidirectional data flow\\n- a declarative UI\\n\\nThe key differences\\nThe main differences show themselves at the platform level:\\n- Rendering\\n- React → the browser\'s DOM\\n- React Native → native UI components (`View`, `Text`)\\n\\n- Styling\\n- there is no CSS\\n- a JS object with styles is used\\n- a limited set of properties\\n\\n- Access to the platform\\n- React Native works with:\\n- the camera\\n- geolocation\\n- native APIs\\n\\n- through a bridge or native modules\\n\\n- The ecosystem\\n- different libraries\\n- its own build tools\\n- its own debugging specifics\\n\\nWhen React Native is used\\n\\n- cross-platform mobile applications\\n- small and medium projects\\n- teams with experience in React\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Native uses React\'s philosophy but solves a different problem. It is mobile development with a React approach, not simply \\"the web without a browser\\".","const styles = {\\n  container: {\\n    flex: 1,\\n    padding: 16,\\n  },\\n}"],[2190,"`useState` is for holding and changing state inside a functional component. It lets React track changes in the data and re-render the component. The state is preserved between renders. Without `useState` a component would be static. It is one of React\'s basic hooks.","State is a key concept in React, and `useState` is the main tool for working with it.\\n\\nWhat state is in React\\nState is data that:\\n- affects the display of the UI\\n- can change over time\\n- causes a repeated render when it changes\\n\\nHow useState works\\n`useState` returns a pair of values:\\n- the current state\\n- a function for updating it\\n\\nWhen `setCount` is called:\\n- the state is updated\\n- React starts a repeated render\\n- the UI is synchronized with the data\\n\\nWhen to use useState\\n\\n- user input\\n- toggles\\n- local UI state\\n- a component\'s temporary data\\n\\nWhen not to use it\\n\\n- computed values\\n- data arriving from props\\n- the application\'s global state\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useState` is the basic mechanism for managing local state. It makes functional components dynamic and reactive.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0)"],[2191,"`useRef` lets you obtain a reference to a DOM element and address it directly. A ref\'s value is preserved between renders and causes no repaint when it changes. This is convenient for focus, for measurements and for integrating with native APIs. `useRef` is often used where React state would be excessive. It works as a container for a mutable value.","Sometimes React state is not the best tool, especially when you need to work with the DOM directly.\\n\\nWhat useRef is\\nuseRef is a hook that returns an object of the form:\\n- `{ current: ... }`\\n- the value is preserved between renders\\n- changing `current` does not cause a re-render\\n\\nWorking with DOM elements\\nThe frequent scenario is access to a real DOM node.\\n\\nThis approach is used for:\\n- setting focus\\n- scrolling\\n- measuring the sizes of elements\\n- working with a canvas or a video\\n\\nuseRef as a store for a value\\nBesides the DOM, `useRef` is used for:\\n- holding timers\\n- holding previous values\\n- flags that do not affect the UI\\n\\nWhen useRef is preferable to useState\\n\\n- the value takes no part in the render\\n- it changes frequently\\n- it matters to avoid unnecessary repaints\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useRef` is a safe way of working with the DOM and of holding mutable values without affecting a component\'s rendering.","const inputRef = useRef(null)\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  inputRef.current.focus()\\n}, [])\\n\\nreturn <input ref={inputRef} />"],[2192,"`useMemo` and `useCallback` have a cost of their own. They complicate the code and increase the cognitive load. Used incorrectly they give no gain in performance. Sometimes they even slow an application down. These hooks have to be applied deliberately and selectively.","Memoization in React is an optimization tool, not a default rule.\\n\\nWhat the cost of useMemo and useCallback is\\nEach of these hooks:\\n- holds values in memory\\n- compares the dependencies\\n- complicates the reading of the code\\n\\nThat only makes sense when the gain exceeds the cost.\\n\\nThe typical problems when they are overused\\n\\n- Unnecessary complexity\\n- more code\\n- harder to read and to maintain\\n- more mistakes in the dependencies\\n\\n- The absence of any real gain\\n- cheap computations\\n- simple functions\\n- infrequent re-renders\\n\\n- A false sense of optimization\\n- the code looks \\"optimized\\"\\n- but there was no real problem\\n\\nWhen useMemo and useCallback are justified\\n\\n- expensive computations\\n- stable references for memoized components\\n- optimizing lists and tables\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useMemo` and `useCallback` are useful only when there is a measurable problem. Without profiling they often do more harm than good.","const value = useMemo(() => count + 1, [count]) // pointless"],[2193,"Custom hooks are used to move repeating logic out of components. They make the code simpler and improve reusability. Often it is work with an API, with events, with state or with browser APIs. Custom hooks do not render UI but manage logic. They are the main means of composition in React.","Custom hooks let you abstract logic without using HOCs or render props.\\n\\nWhy custom hooks are needed\\nThey solve several problems:\\n- they reduce code duplication\\n- they make components cleaner\\n- they make the logic easier to test\\n\\nThe typical examples of custom hooks\\nIn practice the following options are often encountered.\\n- Working with data\\n- loading data\\n- handling the loading / error states\\n\\n- Working with browser APIs\\n- `localStorage`\\n- `window.resize`\\n- `IntersectionObserver`\\n\\n- UI logic\\n- managing modals\\n- toggles\\n- debounce / throttle\\n\\n- State and effects\\n- timers\\n- subscriptions\\n- polling\\n\\nThe rules of a good custom hook\\n\\n- it does not depend on the UI\\n- it has a clear API\\n- it solves one problem\\n- it is easy to test\\n\\nConclusion\\nCustom hooks are the main tool for reusing logic in React. They make code more modular and more maintainable.","function useFetch(url) {\\n  // loading the data, handling the states\\n}"],[2194,"React requires a single root element because every component has to return one tree of elements. That makes building the Virtual DOM and the process of comparing changes easier. A single root makes a component\'s structure predictable. Where necessary you can use a `Fragment`. This restriction follows from React\'s internal architecture.","The restriction to a single root element follows from how React represents the UI internally.\\n\\nHow React sees a component\\nEvery component:\\n- returns a tree of elements\\n- has a single entry point\\n- takes part in the Virtual DOM comparison\\n\\nReact expects a single root value in order to build the tree properly.\\n\\nWhat would happen without a single root\\nIf a component could return several roots:\\n- comparing the changes would be harder\\n- reconciliation would become more complicated\\n- the integrity of the tree would be broken\\n\\nHow the problem is solved\\nFor convenience React provides several solutions.\\n- A wrapping element\\n- `div`\\n- `section`\\n\\n- A Fragment\\n- it adds no unnecessary DOM node\\n- it preserves the logical grouping\\n\\nWhy this is not merely a restriction\\nA single root:\\n- makes React\'s algorithms simpler\\n- makes components\' behaviour stable\\n- reduces the number of edge cases\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe requirement of a single root element is a consequence of React\'s architecture. A `Fragment` lets you keep to that rule without unnecessary DOM nodes.","<>\\n  <Header />\\n  <Content />\\n</>"],[2195,"Loading data is normally done in effects after the component has mounted. `useEffect` or special libraries are used. The loading, data and error state are held separately. It is important to handle repeated requests and cancellation. This approach makes a component predictable.","Loading data is one of the most frequent tasks in frontend.\\n\\nThe basic approach\\nThe classic way of loading data looks like this:\\n- start the request on mounting\\n- save the result in the state\\n- display loading and error\\n\\nThe typical states\\nA component normally holds:\\n- `loading`\\n- `data`\\n- `error`\\n\\nThat lets you control the UI according to the state of the request.\\n\\nThe problems with the manual approach\\n\\n- code duplication\\n- the difficulty of handling race conditions\\n- cancelling requests on unmount\\n\\nThe modern solutions\\nThat is why people often use:\\n- libraries for managing server state\\n- automatic caching\\n- repeated requests and refetching\\n\\nConclusion\\nLoading data in React is built around effects and state. For complex scenarios it is better to use specialized tools.","useEffect(() => {\\n  // fetching the data and updating the state\\n}, [])"],[2196,"Normally a separate layer for working with the API is created. The requests are isolated from the components and reused. The loading and error states are handled. Abstractions over `fetch` or libraries are used. This makes maintenance and testing easier.","Working with an API is not simply a `fetch` call but part of an application\'s architecture.\\n\\nThe basic principles of working with an API\\nGood practice includes:\\n- a separate API layer\\n- a single way of handling errors\\n- predictable data formats\\n\\nThe typical organization\\nMost often:\\n- An API client\\n- a wrapper over `fetch`\\n- configuring the base URL\\n- adding the headers\\n\\n- Request functions\\n- one request — one function\\n- the reuse of logic\\n- explicit data types\\n\\nIntegration with the components\\n\\n- the components know nothing of the API\'s details\\n- the UI responds to the state of the request\\n- the data arrives already in a convenient format\\n\\nWhy this is better\\n\\n- less duplication\\n- the API is easier to change\\n- the code is easier to test\\n\\nConclusion\\nA dedicated layer for working with the API makes frontend code cleaner and more resilient to changes in the backend.","function getUsers() {\\n  return api.get(\'/users\')\\n}"],[2197,"React Query takes on the loading, the caching and the updating of server data. It manages the `loading`, `error` and `success` states automatically. The library saves you from a manual `useEffect` and from holding the data in `useState`. The data becomes consistent across the whole application. This makes components\' code considerably simpler.","Server state is different in nature from local UI state and calls for a separate approach.\\n\\nWhat server state is\\nServer state is data that:\\n- comes from the server\\n- can go stale\\n- can change outside the application\\n- requires synchronization\\n\\nHolding such data in `useState` is awkward and dangerous.\\n\\nWhat React Query does\\nReact Query takes on the key tasks:\\n- Loading the data\\n- an automatic request on mounting\\n- repeated requests where necessary\\n\\n- Caching\\n- a shared cache for the whole application\\n- the reuse of data between components\\n\\n- Managing the states\\n- `isLoading`\\n- `isError`\\n- `data`\\n\\nThe additional capabilities\\n\\n- automatic refetching\\n- background updates\\n- invalidating the cache after mutations\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Query removes most of the routine work with server state. It makes code simpler, more stable and easier to maintain.","const { data, isLoading } = useQuery([\'users\'], fetchUsers)"],[2198,"With a manual `fetch` you have to manage the cache, the states and the repeated requests yourself. React Query automates those tasks. It prevents duplicated requests and data getting out of sync. Handling errors and repeated loads also becomes easier. This makes an application more reliable.","A manual `fetch` seems simple, but it quickly leads to complicated code.\\n\\nThe problems with a manual fetch\\nWhen using `fetch` directly the following difficulties arise.\\n- Duplicated logic\\n- `loading`\\n- `error`\\n- holding the data\\n- repeated requests\\n\\n- The absence of a cache\\n- identical requests are performed several times\\n- an unnecessary load on the server\\n- inconsistent data\\n\\n- Race conditions\\n- stale responses\\n- the difficulty of cancelling requests\\n- bugs when switching screens quickly\\n\\n- Complicated refetching\\n- updating the data after mutations\\n- synchronizing the state by hand\\n\\nWhat React Query gives you\\nReact Query solves these problems by means of:\\n- a global cache\\n- the deduplication of requests\\n- automatic synchronization\\n- a declarative API\\n\\nThe practical effect\\n\\n- less code\\n- fewer bugs\\n- more predictable behaviour\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Query takes the work with server data to a higher level of abstraction. It eliminates most of the problems with a manual `fetch` and improves an application\'s stability.","useMutation(updateUser, {\\n  onSuccess: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries([\'users\']),\\n})"],[2199,"The setter from `useState` can take either a new value or an updater function. The updater function receives the previous state and returns the new one. The second option is needed when the new state depends on the previous one. React decides for itself when to apply the update.","`useState` returns the current state and a function for updating it.\\n\\nA definition\\nThe state setter is a function that tells React the state needs updating.\\n\\nPassing a value\\nUsed when the new state does not depend on the old one.\\n\\nPassing a function (an updater)\\nUsed when the new value depends on the previous one.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- State updates can be grouped (batching).\\n- The value of `count` inside the closure may be stale.\\n- The updater function always receives the current state.\\n\\nAn example of the problem without an updater\\n\\nThe right version\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe `useState` setter takes either a value or a function, and when there is a dependence on the previous state an updater function must always be used.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nsetCount(5);\\n\\nsetCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n\\nsetCount(count + 1);\\nsetCount(count + 1);\\n// the result may be +1 rather than +2\\n\\nsetCount(c => c + 1);\\nsetCount(c => c + 1);\\n// guaranteed +2"],[2200,"Passing a value to `setState` substitutes the new state directly, whereas a callback (an updater function) computes it from the previous one. A callback guarantees correctness across several updates in a row. That is especially important with asynchronous events and with the batching of updates. In React hooks a callback is the preferred way when there is a dependence on the old state.","Although both options update the state, they behave differently in complex scenarios.\\n\\nA definition\\nA callback in setState is a function that receives the previous state and returns the new one.\\n\\nPassing a value\\n\\nThe problem\\n\\n- `count` may be stale.\\n- Several calls may be combined.\\n\\nThe callback version\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- The state is always current.\\n- It is safe with batching.\\n- It works correctly in asynchronous callbacks.\\n\\nWhen to use a callback\\n\\n- The new state depends on the previous one.\\n- There are several updates in a row.\\n- Asynchronous events (timers, promises).\\n\\nConclusion\\nIf the new state depends on the old one, always use the callback form of `setState`; it is more reliable and more predictable.","setCount(count + 1);\\n\\nsetCount(prev => prev + 1);"]]');export{e as default};